window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-12-2", "source": "2023年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “Nowadays parents are increasingly aware that allowing kids more freedom to explore and learn on their own helps foster their independence and boost their confidence.” You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay.You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words(not including the sentence given).", "sample": "Nowadays parents are increasingly aware that allowing kids more freedom to explore and learn on their own\nhelps foster their independence and boost their confidence. This shift in parenting reflects a valuable truth:\nchildren grow strongest not when every step is arranged for them, but when they are trusted to try, fail, and\ntry again.\nTo begin with, freedom teaches independence in a way no lecture ever could. A child who is allowed to plan a\nweekend trip, cook a simple meal, or solve a quarrel with friends gradually learns to rely on his own judgment.\nTake my younger cousin, for example. When his mother stopped correcting his every homework mistake and\nlet him check the answers himself, his grades slowly improved, and, more importantly, he began tackling new\nproblems without fear. Each small success became a brick building his confidence.\nIn this way, granting children the room to explore freely is not a risk but a wise investment. As a result, they\nmature into adults who dare to think, decide, and stand on their own feet."}, "translation": {"prompt": "随着经济与社会的发展，中国人口结构发生了显著变化，逐渐步入老龄化社会。中国老年人口将持续增加，人口老龄化趋势将更加明显。为了应对人口老龄化带来的种种挑战，国家正积极采取措施，加大对养老的支持。通过改革社会保障(social security)制度，政府不断增加社会保障经费，逐步扩大社会保障覆盖范围，使更多老年人受益。政府还鼓励各种社会团体为老年人提供服务。在政府和社会团体的共同努力下，老年人将生活得更加幸福。", "sample": "With the development of the economy and society, China’s population structure has undergone remarkable changes and is gradually entering an aging society. China’s elderly population will continue to increase, and the trend of population aging will become more pronounced. To cope with the various challenges brought by population aging, the country is actively taking measures to increase its support for elderly care. By reforming the social security system, the government keeps increasing social security funding and gradually expanding its coverage, so that more elderly people can benefit. In addition, the government encourages various social organizations to provide services for the elderly. With the joint efforts of the government and social organizations, the elderly will live a happier life."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2023-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Our brains respond to language expressing facts differently than they do to words conveying possibility, scientists at New York University have recently found. Their work offers new insights into the impact word choice has on how we  __26__  between statements expressing what is real versus what is merely possible. The researchers assert their findings are important because we are presented with false information all the time. Some of this is  __27__  , as is the case with deceptive advertisements, but the problem is  __28__  by individuals who believe they are sharing correct information. Thus, it is more important than ever to separate the factual from the possible or merely  __29__  in how we communicate. This is especially true as the study makes clear that information presented as fact  __30__  special responses in our brains, which are distinct from when we process the same content with clear indicators of  __31__  .\nIn their new study, the scientists intended to  __32__  how the brain computes possibilities as expressed by words such as \"may,\" \"might,\" and \"if.\" The researchers compared brain responses to statements expressing factual  __33__  and those expressing possibility. \"There is a monster under my bed\" exemplifies a factual statement. \"I will stay home,\" is also factual. This is opposed to statements that express possibility, like \"There might be a monster under my bed,\" or \"If it rains, I will stay home.\" The results of the study showed that factual language  __34__  a rapid increase in brain activity, with the brain responding more powerfully and showing more engagement with factual phrases compared to those communicating possibility. Thus, facts rule when it comes to the brain. Brain regions involved in processing  __35__  rapidly distinguish facts from possibilities. Further, these regions respond in a much more robust fashion to factual statements.", "matchIntro": "Treasure Fever", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Most visitors come to Cape Canaveral, on the northeast coast of Florida, for the tourist attractions. It's home to the second-busiest cruise ship port in the world and is a gateway to the cosmos. Nearly 1.5 million visitors flock here every year to watch rockets, spacecraft, and satellites blast off into the solar system from Kennedy Space Center Visitor Complex. Nearly 64 kilometers of undeveloped beach and 648 square kilometers of protected refuge fan out from the cape's sandy shores."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Yet some of Cape Canaveral's most legendary attractions lie unseen, wedged under the sea's surface in mud and sand, for this part of the world has a reputation as a deadly ship trap. Over the centuries, dozens of majestic Old World sailing ships smashed and sank on this irregular stretch of windy Florida coast. They were vessels built for war and commerce, crossing the globe carrying everything from coins to cannons, boxes of silver and gold, chests of jewels and porcelain, and pearls from the Caribbean."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cape Canaveral contains one of the greatest concentrations of colonial shipwrecks in the world. In recent years, advances in radar, diving, detection equipment, computers, and GPS have transformed the hunt. The naked eye might see a pile of rocks, but technology can reveal the precious artifacts(人工制品) that lie hidden on the ocean floor."}, {"label": "D", "text": "As technology renders the seabed more accessible, the hunt for treasure-filled ships has drawn a fresh tide of salvors(打捞人员) and their investors—as well as marine archaeologists(考古学家) wanting to bring to light the lost relics. But of late, when salvors have found vessels, their rights have been challenged in court. The big question: who should have control of these treasures?"}, {"label": "E", "text": "High-stakes fights over shipwrecks pit archaeologists against treasure hunters in a vicious cycle of accusations. Archaeologists regard themselves as protectors of history, and they see salvors as careless destroyers. Salvors feel they do the hard work of searching for ships, only to have them stolen from under them when discovered. This kind of clash inevitably takes place on a grand scale. Aside from the salvors, their investors, and the maritime archaeologists who serve as expert witnesses, the battles sweep in local and international governments and organizations like UNESCO that work to protect under-water heritage. The court cases that ensue stretch on for years. Are finders keepers, or do the ships belong to the countries that made them and sent them sailing centuries ago? Where once salvors and archaeologists worked side by side, now they belong to opposing, and equally contemptuous, tribes."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Nearly three million vessels lie wrecked on the Earth's ocean floor—from old canoes to the Titanic—and likely less than one percent have been explored. Some—like an ancient Roman ship found off Antikythera, Greece, dated between 70 and 60 BC and carrying astonishingly sophisticated gears and dials for navigating by the sun—are critical to a new understanding of our past. No wonder there is an eternal stirring among everybody from salvors to scholars to find them."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In May 2016, a salvor named Bobby Pritchett, president of Global Marine Exploration (GME) in Tampa, Florida, announced that he had discovered scattered remains of a ship buried a kilometer off Cape Canaveral. Over the prior three years, he and his crew had obtained 14 state permits to survey a nearly 260-square-kilometer area off the cape; they worked 250 days a year, backed by investor funds of, he claims, US $4 million. It was hard work. Crew members were up at dawn, dragging sensors from their expedition vessels back and forth, day in and day out, year after year, to detect metal of any kind. Using computer technology, Pritchett and his crew created intricate, color-coded maps marked with the GPS coordinates of thousands of finds, all invisible under a meter of sand."}, {"label": "H", "text": "One day in 2015, the magnetometer(磁力计) picked up metal that turned out to be an iron cannon; when the divers blew the sand away, they also discovered a more precious bronze cannon with markings indicating French royalty and, not far off, a famous marble column carved with the coat of arms of France, known from historical paintings. The discovery was cause for celebration. The artifacts indicated the divers had likely found the wreck of La Trinité, a 16th-century French vessel that had been at the center of a bloody battle between France and Spain that changed the fate of the United States of America."}, {"label": "I", "text": "And then the legal storm began, with GME and Pritchett pitted against Florida and France. The Sunken Military Craft Act of 2004, a US federal act, protects any vessel that was on a military mission, allowing the originating country to claim their ship even centuries later. In 2018, two long years after Pritchett's discovery, the federal district court ruled in favor of France. For Pritchett, the decision was devastating. Millions of dollars of investor funding and years of labor were lost."}, {"label": "J", "text": "But this is far from the first time a salvor has lost all rights to a discovery. In 2012, for instance, Spain won a five-year legal battle against Odyssey Marine Exploration, which had hauled 594,000 gold and silver coins from a Spanish wreck off the coast of Portugal across the Atlantic to the United States. \"Treasure hunters can be naive,\" says attorney David Concannon, who has had several maritime archaeologists as clients and represented two sides in the battles over the Titanic for 20 years. \"Many treasure hunters don't understand they are going to have to fight for their rights against a government that has an endless supply of money for legal battles that treasure hunters are likely to lose.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Putting an inflated price on artifacts rather than viewing them as cultural and historical treasures that transcend any price is what irritates many archaeologists. For the archaeologist, everything in a wreck matters—hair, fabric, a fragment of a newspaper, rat bones-all things speak volumes. Archaeologists don't want artifacts ending up in a private collection instead of taking humanity on a journey of understanding."}, {"label": "L", "text": "George Bass is one of the pioneers of under-water archaeology, and a researcher at Texas A&M University. He has testified in court against treasure hunters, but says archaeology is not without its own serious problems. He believes archaeologists need to do a better job themselves instead of routinely criticizing treasure hunters. \"Archaeology has a terrible reputation for not publishing enough on its excavations(发掘) and finds,\" he says. Gathering data, unearthing and meticulously preserving and examining finds, verifying identity and origin, piecing together the larger story, and writing and publishing a comprehensive paper or book can take decades. A bit cynically, Bass describes colleagues who never published because they waited so long they became ill or died. Who is more at fault, Bass asks, the professional archaeologist who carefully excavates a site and never publishes on it or the treasure hunter who locates a submerged wreck, salvages part, conserves part, and publishes a book on the operation?"}, {"label": "M", "text": "Pritchett concedes that his find deserves careful excavation and preservation. \"I think what I found should go in a museum,\" he says. \"But I also think I should get paid for what I found.\" Indeed, it's a bit of a mystery why governments, archaeologists, and treasure hunters can't work together—and why salvors aren't at least given a substantial finder's fee before the original owner takes possession of the vessel and its artifacts."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Could you get by without using the internet for four and a half years? That's exactly what singer and actress Selena Gomez has done in a bid to improve her mental health.\nShe has spoken extensively about the relationship between her social media usage and her mental wellbeing, recalling feeling like \"an addict\" when she became Instagram's most followed user in 2016. \"Taking a break from social media was the best decision that I've ever made for my mental health\", says she. \"The unnecessary hate and comparisons went away once I put my phone down.\"\nDitching the web at large, however, is a far more subtle and complicated prospect. The increasing digitisation of our society means that everything from paying a gas bill to plotting a route to a friend's house and even making a phone call is at the mercy of your internet connection. Actively opting out of using the internet becomes a matter of privilege.\nMs Gomez's multi-millionaire status has allowed her to take the \"social\" out of social media, so she can continue to leverage her enormous fame while keeping the trolls(恶意挑衅的帖子) at bay. The fact that she's still the second most-followed woman on Instagram suggests it's entirely possible to maintain a significant web profile to promote various projects—by way of a dedicated team—without being exposed to the cruel comments, hate mail and rape or death threats.\nIt goes without saying that this is fundamentally different from how the rest of us without beauty deals and films to publicise use the likes of Instagram, TikTok and Twitter, but even the concept of a digital detox(戒瘾) requires having a device and connectivity to choose to disconnect from.\nThe UK's digital divide has worsened over the past two years, leaving poorer families without broadband connections in their homes. Digital exclusion is a major threat to wider societal equality in the UK, so witnessing companies like Facebook championing the metaverse(元宇宙) as the next great frontier when school children are struggling to complete their homework feels particularly irritating.\nConsequently, it's worth bearing in mind that while deleting all social media accounts will undoubtedly make some feel infinitely better, many other people benefit from the strong sense of community that sharing platforms can breed.\nInternet access will continue to grow in importance as we edge further towards web 3.0, and greater resources and initiatives are needed to provide the underprivileged with the connectivity they desperately need to learn, work and live. It's crucial that people who feel that social media is having a detrimental effect on their mental health are allowed to switch off and for those living in digital exclusion to be able to switch on in the first place.", "47": "Could you get by without using the internet for four and a half years? That's exactly what singer and actress Selena Gomez has done in a bid to improve her mental health.\nShe has spoken extensively about the relationship between her social media usage and her mental wellbeing, recalling feeling like \"an addict\" when she became Instagram's most followed user in 2016. \"Taking a break from social media was the best decision that I've ever made for my mental health\", says she. \"The unnecessary hate and comparisons went away once I put my phone down.\"\nDitching the web at large, however, is a far more subtle and complicated prospect. The increasing digitisation of our society means that everything from paying a gas bill to plotting a route to a friend's house and even making a phone call is at the mercy of your internet connection. Actively opting out of using the internet becomes a matter of privilege.\nMs Gomez's multi-millionaire status has allowed her to take the \"social\" out of social media, so she can continue to leverage her enormous fame while keeping the trolls(恶意挑衅的帖子) at bay. The fact that she's still the second most-followed woman on Instagram suggests it's entirely possible to maintain a significant web profile to promote various projects—by way of a dedicated team—without being exposed to the cruel comments, hate mail and rape or death threats.\nIt goes without saying that this is fundamentally different from how the rest of us without beauty deals and films to publicise use the likes of Instagram, TikTok and Twitter, but even the concept of a digital detox(戒瘾) requires having a device and connectivity to choose to disconnect from.\nThe UK's digital divide has worsened over the past two years, leaving poorer families without broadband connections in their homes. Digital exclusion is a major threat to wider societal equality in the UK, so witnessing companies like Facebook championing the metaverse(元宇宙) as the next great frontier when school children are struggling to complete their homework feels particularly irritating.\nConsequently, it's worth bearing in mind that while deleting all social media accounts will undoubtedly make some feel infinitely better, many other people benefit from the strong sense of community that sharing platforms can breed.\nInternet access will continue to grow in importance as we edge further towards web 3.0, and greater resources and initiatives are needed to provide the underprivileged with the connectivity they desperately need to learn, work and live. It's crucial that people who feel that social media is having a detrimental effect on their mental health are allowed to switch off and for those living in digital exclusion to be able to switch on in the first place.", "48": "Could you get by without using the internet for four and a half years? That's exactly what singer and actress Selena Gomez has done in a bid to improve her mental health.\nShe has spoken extensively about the relationship between her social media usage and her mental wellbeing, recalling feeling like \"an addict\" when she became Instagram's most followed user in 2016. \"Taking a break from social media was the best decision that I've ever made for my mental health\", says she. \"The unnecessary hate and comparisons went away once I put my phone down.\"\nDitching the web at large, however, is a far more subtle and complicated prospect. The increasing digitisation of our society means that everything from paying a gas bill to plotting a route to a friend's house and even making a phone call is at the mercy of your internet connection. Actively opting out of using the internet becomes a matter of privilege.\nMs Gomez's multi-millionaire status has allowed her to take the \"social\" out of social media, so she can continue to leverage her enormous fame while keeping the trolls(恶意挑衅的帖子) at bay. The fact that she's still the second most-followed woman on Instagram suggests it's entirely possible to maintain a significant web profile to promote various projects—by way of a dedicated team—without being exposed to the cruel comments, hate mail and rape or death threats.\nIt goes without saying that this is fundamentally different from how the rest of us without beauty deals and films to publicise use the likes of Instagram, TikTok and Twitter, but even the concept of a digital detox(戒瘾) requires having a device and connectivity to choose to disconnect from.\nThe UK's digital divide has worsened over the past two years, leaving poorer families without broadband connections in their homes. Digital exclusion is a major threat to wider societal equality in the UK, so witnessing companies like Facebook championing the metaverse(元宇宙) as the next great frontier when school children are struggling to complete their homework feels particularly irritating.\nConsequently, it's worth bearing in mind that while deleting all social media accounts will undoubtedly make some feel infinitely better, many other people benefit from the strong sense of community that sharing platforms can breed.\nInternet access will continue to grow in importance as we edge further towards web 3.0, and greater resources and initiatives are needed to provide the underprivileged with the connectivity they desperately need to learn, work and live. It's crucial that people who feel that social media is having a detrimental effect on their mental health are allowed to switch off and for those living in digital exclusion to be able to switch on in the first place.", "49": "Could you get by without using the internet for four and a half years? That's exactly what singer and actress Selena Gomez has done in a bid to improve her mental health.\nShe has spoken extensively about the relationship between her social media usage and her mental wellbeing, recalling feeling like \"an addict\" when she became Instagram's most followed user in 2016. \"Taking a break from social media was the best decision that I've ever made for my mental health\", says she. \"The unnecessary hate and comparisons went away once I put my phone down.\"\nDitching the web at large, however, is a far more subtle and complicated prospect. The increasing digitisation of our society means that everything from paying a gas bill to plotting a route to a friend's house and even making a phone call is at the mercy of your internet connection. Actively opting out of using the internet becomes a matter of privilege.\nMs Gomez's multi-millionaire status has allowed her to take the \"social\" out of social media, so she can continue to leverage her enormous fame while keeping the trolls(恶意挑衅的帖子) at bay. The fact that she's still the second most-followed woman on Instagram suggests it's entirely possible to maintain a significant web profile to promote various projects—by way of a dedicated team—without being exposed to the cruel comments, hate mail and rape or death threats.\nIt goes without saying that this is fundamentally different from how the rest of us without beauty deals and films to publicise use the likes of Instagram, TikTok and Twitter, but even the concept of a digital detox(戒瘾) requires having a device and connectivity to choose to disconnect from.\nThe UK's digital divide has worsened over the past two years, leaving poorer families without broadband connections in their homes. Digital exclusion is a major threat to wider societal equality in the UK, so witnessing companies like Facebook championing the metaverse(元宇宙) as the next great frontier when school children are struggling to complete their homework feels particularly irritating.\nConsequently, it's worth bearing in mind that while deleting all social media accounts will undoubtedly make some feel infinitely better, many other people benefit from the strong sense of community that sharing platforms can breed.\nInternet access will continue to grow in importance as we edge further towards web 3.0, and greater resources and initiatives are needed to provide the underprivileged with the connectivity they desperately need to learn, work and live. It's crucial that people who feel that social media is having a detrimental effect on their mental health are allowed to switch off and for those living in digital exclusion to be able to switch on in the first place.", "50": "Could you get by without using the internet for four and a half years? That's exactly what singer and actress Selena Gomez has done in a bid to improve her mental health.\nShe has spoken extensively about the relationship between her social media usage and her mental wellbeing, recalling feeling like \"an addict\" when she became Instagram's most followed user in 2016. \"Taking a break from social media was the best decision that I've ever made for my mental health\", says she. \"The unnecessary hate and comparisons went away once I put my phone down.\"\nDitching the web at large, however, is a far more subtle and complicated prospect. The increasing digitisation of our society means that everything from paying a gas bill to plotting a route to a friend's house and even making a phone call is at the mercy of your internet connection. Actively opting out of using the internet becomes a matter of privilege.\nMs Gomez's multi-millionaire status has allowed her to take the \"social\" out of social media, so she can continue to leverage her enormous fame while keeping the trolls(恶意挑衅的帖子) at bay. The fact that she's still the second most-followed woman on Instagram suggests it's entirely possible to maintain a significant web profile to promote various projects—by way of a dedicated team—without being exposed to the cruel comments, hate mail and rape or death threats.\nIt goes without saying that this is fundamentally different from how the rest of us without beauty deals and films to publicise use the likes of Instagram, TikTok and Twitter, but even the concept of a digital detox(戒瘾) requires having a device and connectivity to choose to disconnect from.\nThe UK's digital divide has worsened over the past two years, leaving poorer families without broadband connections in their homes. Digital exclusion is a major threat to wider societal equality in the UK, so witnessing companies like Facebook championing the metaverse(元宇宙) as the next great frontier when school children are struggling to complete their homework feels particularly irritating.\nConsequently, it's worth bearing in mind that while deleting all social media accounts will undoubtedly make some feel infinitely better, many other people benefit from the strong sense of community that sharing platforms can breed.\nInternet access will continue to grow in importance as we edge further towards web 3.0, and greater resources and initiatives are needed to provide the underprivileged with the connectivity they desperately need to learn, work and live. It's crucial that people who feel that social media is having a detrimental effect on their mental health are allowed to switch off and for those living in digital exclusion to be able to switch on in the first place.", "51": "Psychologists have long been in disagreement as to whether competition is a learned or a genetic component of human behavior. Whatever it is, you cannot but recognize the effect competition is exerting in academics and many other areas of contemporary life.\nPsychologically speaking, competition has been seen as an inevitable consequence of human drives. According to Sigmund Freud, humans are born screaming for attention and full of organic drives for fulfillment in various areas. Initially, we compete for the attention of our parents. Thereafter, we are at the mercy of a battle between our base impulses for self-fulfillment and social and cultural norms which prohibit pure indulgence.\nCurrent work in anthropology(人类学) has suggested, however, that this view of the role of competition in human behavior may be incorrect. Thomas Hobbes, one of the great philosophers of the seventeenth century, is perhaps best remembered for his characterization of the \"natural world,\" that is, the world before the imposition of the will of humanity, as being \"nasty, brutish, and short.\" This image of the pre-rational world is still widely held, reinforced by Charles Darwin's highly influential work, The Origin of Species, which established the doctrine of natural selection. This doctrine, which takes for granted that those species best able to adapt to and master the natural environment in which they live will survive, has suggested that the struggle for survival is an inherent human trait which determines a person's success. Darwin's theory has even been summarized as \"survival of the fittest\"—a phrase Darwin himself never used—further highlighting competition's role in success. As it has often been pointed out, however, there is nothing in the concept of natural selection that suggests that competition is the most successful strategy for \"survival of the fittest.\" Darwin asserted in The Origin of Species that the struggles he was describing should be viewed as metaphors and could easily include dependence and cooperation.\nMany studies have been conducted to test the importance placed on competition as opposed to other values, such as cooperation—by various cultures, and generally conclude that Americans uniquely praise competition as natural, inevitable, and desirable. In 1937, the world-renowned anthropologist Margaret Mead published Cooperation and Competition among Primitive Peoples, based on her studies of several societies that did not prize competition, and, in fact, seemed at times to place a negative value on it. One such society was the Zuni Indians of Arizona, and they, Mead found, valued cooperation far more than competition. After studying dozens of such cultures, Mead's final conclusion was that competitiveness is a culturally created aspect of human behavior, and that its prevalence in a particular society is relative to how that society values it.", "52": "Psychologists have long been in disagreement as to whether competition is a learned or a genetic component of human behavior. Whatever it is, you cannot but recognize the effect competition is exerting in academics and many other areas of contemporary life.\nPsychologically speaking, competition has been seen as an inevitable consequence of human drives. According to Sigmund Freud, humans are born screaming for attention and full of organic drives for fulfillment in various areas. Initially, we compete for the attention of our parents. Thereafter, we are at the mercy of a battle between our base impulses for self-fulfillment and social and cultural norms which prohibit pure indulgence.\nCurrent work in anthropology(人类学) has suggested, however, that this view of the role of competition in human behavior may be incorrect. Thomas Hobbes, one of the great philosophers of the seventeenth century, is perhaps best remembered for his characterization of the \"natural world,\" that is, the world before the imposition of the will of humanity, as being \"nasty, brutish, and short.\" This image of the pre-rational world is still widely held, reinforced by Charles Darwin's highly influential work, The Origin of Species, which established the doctrine of natural selection. This doctrine, which takes for granted that those species best able to adapt to and master the natural environment in which they live will survive, has suggested that the struggle for survival is an inherent human trait which determines a person's success. Darwin's theory has even been summarized as \"survival of the fittest\"—a phrase Darwin himself never used—further highlighting competition's role in success. As it has often been pointed out, however, there is nothing in the concept of natural selection that suggests that competition is the most successful strategy for \"survival of the fittest.\" Darwin asserted in The Origin of Species that the struggles he was describing should be viewed as metaphors and could easily include dependence and cooperation.\nMany studies have been conducted to test the importance placed on competition as opposed to other values, such as cooperation—by various cultures, and generally conclude that Americans uniquely praise competition as natural, inevitable, and desirable. In 1937, the world-renowned anthropologist Margaret Mead published Cooperation and Competition among Primitive Peoples, based on her studies of several societies that did not prize competition, and, in fact, seemed at times to place a negative value on it. One such society was the Zuni Indians of Arizona, and they, Mead found, valued cooperation far more than competition. After studying dozens of such cultures, Mead's final conclusion was that competitiveness is a culturally created aspect of human behavior, and that its prevalence in a particular society is relative to how that society values it.", "53": "Psychologists have long been in disagreement as to whether competition is a learned or a genetic component of human behavior. Whatever it is, you cannot but recognize the effect competition is exerting in academics and many other areas of contemporary life.\nPsychologically speaking, competition has been seen as an inevitable consequence of human drives. According to Sigmund Freud, humans are born screaming for attention and full of organic drives for fulfillment in various areas. Initially, we compete for the attention of our parents. Thereafter, we are at the mercy of a battle between our base impulses for self-fulfillment and social and cultural norms which prohibit pure indulgence.\nCurrent work in anthropology(人类学) has suggested, however, that this view of the role of competition in human behavior may be incorrect. Thomas Hobbes, one of the great philosophers of the seventeenth century, is perhaps best remembered for his characterization of the \"natural world,\" that is, the world before the imposition of the will of humanity, as being \"nasty, brutish, and short.\" This image of the pre-rational world is still widely held, reinforced by Charles Darwin's highly influential work, The Origin of Species, which established the doctrine of natural selection. This doctrine, which takes for granted that those species best able to adapt to and master the natural environment in which they live will survive, has suggested that the struggle for survival is an inherent human trait which determines a person's success. Darwin's theory has even been summarized as \"survival of the fittest\"—a phrase Darwin himself never used—further highlighting competition's role in success. As it has often been pointed out, however, there is nothing in the concept of natural selection that suggests that competition is the most successful strategy for \"survival of the fittest.\" Darwin asserted in The Origin of Species that the struggles he was describing should be viewed as metaphors and could easily include dependence and cooperation.\nMany studies have been conducted to test the importance placed on competition as opposed to other values, such as cooperation—by various cultures, and generally conclude that Americans uniquely praise competition as natural, inevitable, and desirable. In 1937, the world-renowned anthropologist Margaret Mead published Cooperation and Competition among Primitive Peoples, based on her studies of several societies that did not prize competition, and, in fact, seemed at times to place a negative value on it. One such society was the Zuni Indians of Arizona, and they, Mead found, valued cooperation far more than competition. After studying dozens of such cultures, Mead's final conclusion was that competitiveness is a culturally created aspect of human behavior, and that its prevalence in a particular society is relative to how that society values it.", "54": "Psychologists have long been in disagreement as to whether competition is a learned or a genetic component of human behavior. Whatever it is, you cannot but recognize the effect competition is exerting in academics and many other areas of contemporary life.\nPsychologically speaking, competition has been seen as an inevitable consequence of human drives. According to Sigmund Freud, humans are born screaming for attention and full of organic drives for fulfillment in various areas. Initially, we compete for the attention of our parents. Thereafter, we are at the mercy of a battle between our base impulses for self-fulfillment and social and cultural norms which prohibit pure indulgence.\nCurrent work in anthropology(人类学) has suggested, however, that this view of the role of competition in human behavior may be incorrect. Thomas Hobbes, one of the great philosophers of the seventeenth century, is perhaps best remembered for his characterization of the \"natural world,\" that is, the world before the imposition of the will of humanity, as being \"nasty, brutish, and short.\" This image of the pre-rational world is still widely held, reinforced by Charles Darwin's highly influential work, The Origin of Species, which established the doctrine of natural selection. This doctrine, which takes for granted that those species best able to adapt to and master the natural environment in which they live will survive, has suggested that the struggle for survival is an inherent human trait which determines a person's success. Darwin's theory has even been summarized as \"survival of the fittest\"—a phrase Darwin himself never used—further highlighting competition's role in success. As it has often been pointed out, however, there is nothing in the concept of natural selection that suggests that competition is the most successful strategy for \"survival of the fittest.\" Darwin asserted in The Origin of Species that the struggles he was describing should be viewed as metaphors and could easily include dependence and cooperation.\nMany studies have been conducted to test the importance placed on competition as opposed to other values, such as cooperation—by various cultures, and generally conclude that Americans uniquely praise competition as natural, inevitable, and desirable. In 1937, the world-renowned anthropologist Margaret Mead published Cooperation and Competition among Primitive Peoples, based on her studies of several societies that did not prize competition, and, in fact, seemed at times to place a negative value on it. One such society was the Zuni Indians of Arizona, and they, Mead found, valued cooperation far more than competition. After studying dozens of such cultures, Mead's final conclusion was that competitiveness is a culturally created aspect of human behavior, and that its prevalence in a particular society is relative to how that society values it.", "55": "Psychologists have long been in disagreement as to whether competition is a learned or a genetic component of human behavior. Whatever it is, you cannot but recognize the effect competition is exerting in academics and many other areas of contemporary life.\nPsychologically speaking, competition has been seen as an inevitable consequence of human drives. According to Sigmund Freud, humans are born screaming for attention and full of organic drives for fulfillment in various areas. Initially, we compete for the attention of our parents. Thereafter, we are at the mercy of a battle between our base impulses for self-fulfillment and social and cultural norms which prohibit pure indulgence.\nCurrent work in anthropology(人类学) has suggested, however, that this view of the role of competition in human behavior may be incorrect. Thomas Hobbes, one of the great philosophers of the seventeenth century, is perhaps best remembered for his characterization of the \"natural world,\" that is, the world before the imposition of the will of humanity, as being \"nasty, brutish, and short.\" This image of the pre-rational world is still widely held, reinforced by Charles Darwin's highly influential work, The Origin of Species, which established the doctrine of natural selection. This doctrine, which takes for granted that those species best able to adapt to and master the natural environment in which they live will survive, has suggested that the struggle for survival is an inherent human trait which determines a person's success. Darwin's theory has even been summarized as \"survival of the fittest\"—a phrase Darwin himself never used—further highlighting competition's role in success. As it has often been pointed out, however, there is nothing in the concept of natural selection that suggests that competition is the most successful strategy for \"survival of the fittest.\" Darwin asserted in The Origin of Species that the struggles he was describing should be viewed as metaphors and could easily include dependence and cooperation.\nMany studies have been conducted to test the importance placed on competition as opposed to other values, such as cooperation—by various cultures, and generally conclude that Americans uniquely praise competition as natural, inevitable, and desirable. In 1937, the world-renowned anthropologist Margaret Mead published Cooperation and Competition among Primitive Peoples, based on her studies of several societies that did not prize competition, and, in fact, seemed at times to place a negative value on it. One such society was the Zuni Indians of Arizona, and they, Mead found, valued cooperation far more than competition. After studying dozens of such cultures, Mead's final conclusion was that competitiveness is a culturally created aspect of human behavior, and that its prevalence in a particular society is relative to how that society values it."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What has the woman been thinking of doing for this summer?", "options": ["A) Renting a car instead of driving their own.", "B) Surfing online to check out the best deals.", "C) Exploring more summer holiday resorts.", "D) Spending their holidays in a novel way."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Spending their holidays in a novel way.\n【定位】So, I've been thinking, this summer, instead of our usual holiday rental, I'd like to try renting\na camper van and exploring a bit more.\n嗯，我一直在想，今年夏天，不走我们往常租度假屋那一套，我想试试租一辆房车、多走走\n看看。\n【信号】意愿句I'd like to。答案信号是女方直接表达意愿的 I'd like to try renting a camper van\nand exploring a bit more，I'd like to（我想）这类意愿句正是“她想做什么”的落点，紧跟\n在 I've been thinking 之后，开门见山。\n【替换】renting a camper van and exploring a bit more ↔ Spending their holidays in a novel way\n具体做法概括为“新方式”\ninstead of our usual holiday rental ↔ in a novel way\n“不走老一套”=“新方式”\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Renting a car instead of driving their own. 盗用了原文 renting a camper van 里的\nrenting，听到“租”就想选；但她租的是 camper van（房车）不是普通 car，更没说“不开自\n己的车”，是把原词嫁接到一个原文没有的意思上。\n借用原文：renting a camper van\nB) 原词陷阱\nSurfing online to check out the best deals. 里 online 确实在录音里出现过，但那是后文\nCheck it out online 让对方上网看这种生活方式，并非她“这个夏天想做的事”，时间点和意\n图都错位。\n借用原文：Check it out online\nC) 偷换概念\nExploring more summer holiday resorts. 借用了 exploring 和 summer holiday 的字\n眼，听感很熟；但她说的是 exploring a bit more（多走走看看更多地方），从没提“度假\n胜地 resorts”，是把模糊的“多探索”偷换成具体的“逛度假村”。\n借用原文：exploring a bit more", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What do we learn about the man's grandfather from the conversation?", "options": ["A) He once owned a van.", "B) He was well travelled.", "C) He did not find holiday homes appealing.", "D) He did not like to be locked into one place."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He once owned a van.\n【定位】M: I think my grandfather had a van like that. It doesn't sound that appealing to me.\n男：我记得我爷爷有过一辆那样的车。这听着对我没什么吸引力。\n【信号】提到祖父那一句。答案信号就是男方亲口提到祖父的 I think my grandfather had a van\nlike that——题目点名 grandfather，原文唯一讲祖父的就是这句，had a van 直接给出关\n于祖父的事实。\n【替换】my grandfather had a van like that ↔ He once owned a van\nhad a van=owned a van\n【排除】B) 未提及\nHe was well travelled.（他见多识广 / 走过很多地方）原文从未说祖父，是无中生有；对话\n只说祖父有过车，没说他爱旅行，属未提及的凭空拔高。\nC) 张冠李戴\nHe did not find holiday homes appealing. 盗用了原文 It doesn't sound that\nappealing to me 里的 appealing；但说“不吸引人”的是男方本人对房车主意的反应，且嫌\n的是房车不是 holiday homes，把男方的话张冠李戴成祖父的态度。\n借用原文：It doesn't sound that appealing to me\nD) 张冠李戴\nHe did not like to be locked into one place. 盗用了 we wouldn't have to be locked\ninto one place 的 locked into one place；但那是女方在讲房车的好处，主语是“我们”，\n不是祖父的喜好，人物与语境双重错位。\n借用原文：we wouldn't have to be locked into one place", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What can many camper vans do nowadays according to the woman?", "options": ["A) Ensure the safety of passengers.", "B) Drive under any weather condition.", "C) Receive instructions via computers.", "D) Generate their own electricity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Generate their own electricity.\n【定位】I've seen quite a few with solar panels that provide electricity for when you're stopped.\n我见过不少装了太阳能板的，停车时能供电。\n【信号】功能描述provide electricity。答案信号是描述具体功能的 solar panels that provide\nelectricity——provide electricity（提供电力）直接说出房车“能做什么”，是这段功能罗列\n里与选项对得上的那一句。\n【替换】solar panels that provide electricity ↔ Generate their own electricity\n供电=自己发电\n【排除】A) 未提及\nEnsure the safety of passengers. 原文整段讲的是供电、Wi-Fi、看视频这些舒适功能，从\n未提“保障乘客安全”，是凭常识硬加的功能，属未提及。\nB) 原词陷阱\nDrive under any weather condition. 利用了原文 if the weather's not great 的\nweather；但原文说的是“天气不好时可以看流媒体视频”，是娱乐功能，根本不是“任何天气\n都能开车”，把 weather 嫁接到一个原文没有的能力上。\n借用原文：if the weather's not great\nC) 原词陷阱\nReceive instructions via computers. 盗用了原文 you can still use your computer 里的\ncomputer；但原文是“你可以用自己的电脑”，主语是人在用电脑，不是房车“通过电脑接收\n指令”，把人的行为偷换成车的功能。\n借用原文：you can still use your computer", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What idea does the woman find attractive?", "options": ["A) Having one's basic needs covered while away from home.", "B) Enjoying the freedom to choose where to go and work.", "C) Slowing down in one's increasingly hectic life.", "D) Riding one's mountain bike on vacation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） Enjoying the freedom to choose where to go and work.\n【定位】Imagine having that kind of freedom to go anywhere you want and work from anywhere in\nthe world.\n想象一下，拥有那种自由——想去哪儿就去哪儿，还能在世界任何地方工作。\n【信号】向往句Imagine having。答案信号是女方满怀憧憬的 Imagine having that kind of\nfreedom——Imagine having（想象一下拥有）是她表达向往的语气，紧跟其后的\nfreedom 就是她觉得吸引人的那一点。\n【替换】that kind of freedom to go anywhere you want and work from anywhere in the world ↔\nEnjoying the freedom to choose where to go and work\n几乎逐点对应的同义改写\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nHaving one's basic needs covered while away from home. 盗用了原文 the basic\nneeds like toilet and showers are covered；但说在意 basic needs 的是男方（M），不\n是女方，题目问的是女方觉得什么吸引人，人物错位。\n借用原文：the basic needs like toilet and showers are covered\nC) 偷换概念\nSlowing down in one's increasingly hectic life. 借用了原文 Slow down 里的 slow\ndown；但那是女方让激动的男方“别急、慢点说”，是对话节奏用语，根本不是她觉得吸引\n人的生活理念，把口语提醒偷换成人生主张。\n借用原文：Slow down\nD) 张冠李戴\nRiding one's mountain bike on vacation. 盗用了原文 my mountain bike；但提山地车\n的是男方（担心装不装得下），与女方“觉得吸引人”毫无关系，人物和语境都错。\n借用原文：my mountain bike", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman from the man's description?", "options": ["A) She has missed several important appointments lately.", "B) Her job performance has worsened over the past month.", "C) She has lagged behind most of her co-workers in output.", "D) Her physical health has deteriorated these past few weeks."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Her job performance has worsened over the past month.\n【定位】Over the past month or so, it has become apparent that your performance has dipped. You've\nbeen late to work several times these past few weeks, which is very uncharacteristic of you.\nBut what really concerns me is your curtailed output.\n在过去一个月左右，很明显你的表现下滑了。这几周你好几次上班迟到，这很不像你。但真\n正让我担心的是你产出减少了。\n【信号】总起定性句 + 转折强调。男方开口就给总定性 your performance has dipped，又用 But\nwhat really concerns me 的转折把重点压到\"产出减少\"上；\"总起判断句 + But 后的强\n调\"正是答案句信号。\n【替换】your performance has dipped ↔ Her job performance has worsened\ndipped=worsened 同义替换\nOver the past month or so ↔ over the past month\n时间范围对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确说她 late to work several times（多次迟到），A 却说 missed several important\nappointments（错过重要约会），盗用\"several\"和\"迟到\"的影子，把\"上班迟到\"偷换成\"错\n过约会\"，answer非所答。\n借用原文：You've been late to work several times these past few weeks\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文只说她 curtailed output（产出减少），C 用了\"output\"原词，却擅自加上 lagged\nbehind most of her co-workers（落后于大多数同事），原文从没拿她和同事作比较，属\n无中生有的范围扩大。\n借用原文：what really concerns me is your curtailed output\nD) 未提及\n原文谈的是工作\"表现/产出\"下滑，D 却说 physical health has deteriorated（身体健康变\n差），原文男方描述中根本没提她身体生病，属未提及；这与第6题的健康选项呼应，是跨\n题混淆。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman say she's experiencing at the moment?", "options": ["A) Serious health issues.", "B) Disturbance of her mind.", "C) Some problems at home.", "D) Penalty for curtailed output."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Some problems at home.\n【定位】I'm sorry. I'm going through some unfortunate family issues at the moment. It's a personal\nmatter, so if you don't mind, I much prefer we don't get into it.\n对不起，我目前正经历一些不幸的家庭问题。这是私人的事，如果你不介意，我更希望我们\n不要深谈。\n【信号】被问者应答 + I'm going through 自述。男方追问 What's going on? 后，女方的直接应\n答 I'm going through some unfortunate family issues 就是答案句；\"被问者应答\"加上\nI'm going through 这种亲口陈述原因的句式，是双重定位信号。\n【替换】family issues ↔ problems at home\nfamily issues=problems at home 同义替换\nat the moment ↔ Some\n\"some...issues\"对\"Some problems\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\n女方从没说自己有 serious health issues（严重健康问题），健康话题是借第5题 D 项的余\n味来混淆，原文她给的原因是家事不是身体，属未提及。\nB) 过度推断\nDisturbance of her mind（心神不宁）原文从未出现，可能由\"她状态不好\"脑补出\"心理问\n题\"，但女方明说是 family issues、a personal matter，是家事而非精神疾患，属过度推\n断。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 curtailed output 是从男方第2段 curtailed output 原封搬来的原词，但本题问她\"在\n经历什么\"，curtailed output 是\"表现\"不是\"原因\"，且\"Penalty for\"（因…受罚）原文根本\n没有，盗原词来答非所问。\n借用原文：what really concerns me is your curtailed output", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the man say makes his job far easier?", "options": ["A) The woman's work proficiency.", "B) The woman's whole-hearted support.", "C) His engaging personality.", "D) His management capability."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The woman's work proficiency.\n【定位】I personally really value your proficiency at your job. It makes my job far easier.\n我个人非常看重你在工作上的熟练。这让我的工作轻松多了。\n【信号】题干同款短语 makes my job easier。原文 It makes my job far easier 与题干 makes\nhis job far easier 是同一短语，前一句的 your proficiency at your job 就是\"It\"指代的内\n容，定位锁这两句。\n【替换】your proficiency at your job ↔ The woman's work proficiency\nyour=the woman's，视角转换\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\n原文男方说 I personally really value your proficiency（看重你的熟练），B 借\"value/对\n她的肯定\"的影子说成 whole-hearted support（全心全意的支持），但让他工作轻松的是\n她的\"熟练\"不是\"支持\"，原文没说她全力支持他，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：I personally really value your proficiency at your job\nC) 张冠李戴\nHis engaging personality（他迷人的性格）讲的是男方自己的特质，但原文让工作轻松的\n是\"你的熟练\"即女方，C 把归属从女方错挪到男方，且\"魅力\"原文从未出现，属张冠李戴。\nD) 张冠李戴\nHis management capability（他的管理能力）原文从未提及，且方向反了——让工作轻松\n的是女方的熟练，不是男方自己的管理力，既张冠李戴又无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What agreement have the speakers reached by the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) The woman will resume her work in two weeks.", "B) The man will be back at his 100% in a couple of weeks.", "C) The woman will be off work on the next two Mondays.", "D) The man will help the woman get back to her usual self."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The woman will be off work on the next two Mondays.\n【定位】W: Would it be possible to take the next two Mondays off? ... M: Sure thing. ... M: So this is\nwhat we're going to do. You will take those days off, and I expect that you will gradually\ncome back to your usual self.\n女：能让我接下来两个周一请假吗？……男：当然可以。……男：那我们就这么办，你把那两\n天休掉，我希望你慢慢恢复到平时的状态。\n【信号】请求—应允—拍板三处合一。女方提出 take the next two Mondays off，男方 Sure thing\n应允，结尾再 So this is what we're going to do. You will take those days off 拍板；\"请\n求被应允并在结尾确认\"是约定题的答案信号。\n【替换】take the next two Mondays off ↔ be off work on the next two Mondays\ntake...off=be off work 同义替换\nYou will take those days off ↔ The woman will be off work\n结尾确认句坐实主语是女方\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 resume her work in two weeks（两周后复工），但原文女方只是休\"两个周一\"、并\n非停工两周后才回来；\"two\"原词在，时间概念却被偷换，且 in two months 才是再约谈时\n间，A 张冠时间。\n借用原文：take the next two Mondays off\nB) 张冠李戴\nB 说 The man will be back at his 100%，但原文 What I need is for you to be back at\nyour 100% 是男方要求\"你（女方）\"回到100%，主语该是女方不是男方；盗用 100% 原词\n却把人安反，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：What I need is for you to be back at your 100%\nD) 过度推断\nD 说 The man will help the woman get back to her usual self，但原文是男方期望\"你会\n慢慢恢复\"you will gradually come back to your usual self，是女方自己恢复，并非男\n方\"帮\"她；盗 usual self 原词却脑补出\"帮助\"，属过度推断。\n借用原文：you will gradually come back to your usual self", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does research suggest about a higher level of education?", "options": ["A) It can have an impact on our moods and emotions.", "B) It can enable us to live a healthier and longer life.", "C) It can improve our financial status significantly.", "D) It can help us achieve better work performance."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It can enable us to live a healthier and longer life.\n【定位】Research suggests that higher levels of education can lead to better health and even\ngreater longevity.\n研究表明，更高的教育水平能带来更好的健康状况，甚至更长的寿命。\n【信号】题干原词在原文的复现句。录音里 Research suggests that higher levels of education 几\n乎逐字复述了题干 What does research suggest about a higher level of education 的措\n辞，这种'题干怎么问、原文就怎么开头'的句子就是答案句，紧跟其后的 can lead to better\nhealth and even greater longevity 即为结论。\n【替换】better health and even greater longevity ↔ live a healthier and longer life\n名词换成形容词、同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头确实念到 our emotions，但那是在列举'影响健康的诸多因素'时提到的，并非\n说'教育能影响情绪'。选项 have an impact on our moods and emotions 把这个原词搬\n来，答的却不是题干问的'教育带来什么'。\n借用原文：our finances, our emotions and the way we work\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 our finances 同样只是被列为'影响健康的因素'之一，原文从未说'教育能显著改善财\n务'。选项 improve our financial status significantly 借 finances 这个原词设套，与题干\n所问无关。\n借用原文：our finances, our emotions and the way we work\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说 the way we work 是影响健康的因素之一，并未说'教育能带来更好的工作表现'。选\n项 achieve better work performance 用 work 这个原词制造熟悉感，但属无中生有。\n借用原文：the way we work", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do we learn from the finding of the new research?", "options": ["A) One's health tends to differ before and after marriage.", "B) One's health status is related to one's social background.", "C) The wealthier one's spouse is, the healthier one becomes.", "D) The spouse's level of education can impact one's health."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The spouse's level of education can impact one's health.\n【定位】The team found that a person whose spouse had a higher level of education tended to report\nbetter health.\n研究团队发现，配偶受教育水平更高的人，往往报告自己的健康状况更好。\n【信号】The team found 引出研究结论。The team found that 是典型的'结论引导句'，篇章里凡\n以'研究团队发现……'开头的话，几乎必是某道题的答案句；其后整句就是这项新研究的核心\n发现。\n【替换】a person whose spouse had a higher level of education tended to report better health ↔\nThe spouse's level of education can impact one's health\n'配偶教育高→健康好'概括为'配偶教育影响健康'\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 health status before and after marriage 是作者明确列出的'被调整掉的其他因\n素'之一，意在说'即便排除它结论依然成立'，根本不是研究发现。选项 One's health tends\nto differ before and after marriage 反把这个被排除项当成结论。\n借用原文：adjusting for other factors, such as participants' own education, social background and\nhealth status before and after marriage\nB) 原词陷阱\nsocial background 与上一项一样，是被作者 adjusting for 排除掉的干扰因素，原文没\n说'健康与社会背景有关'是新发现。选项 One's health status is related to one's social\nbackground 把被排除项伪装成结论。\n借用原文：adjusting for other factors, such as participants' own education, social background and\nhealth status before and after marriage\nC) 偷换概念\n研究讲的是配偶'教育'影响健康，C 却说 The wealthier one's spouse is, the healthier\none becomes，把'教育'偷换成'财富'。虽然录音后文提到教育能带来 more money 等资\n源，但那是教育的衍生好处，原文从未把'配偶越富→本人越健康'立为结论。\n借用原文：more money", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the passage say about the men in the sample of the new research?", "options": ["A) They benefited a lot from their career achievements.", "B) They showed interest in their spouse's occupations.", "C) They had much in common with their spouses.", "D) They had more education than their spouses."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They had more education than their spouses.\n【定位】Women in this sample had lower levels of education and lower occupational achievements\nthan the men.\n该样本中的女性，其受教育水平和职业成就都低于男性。\n【信号】比较句 lower…than the men 反推男性更高。答案句以女性为主语作比较——Women in\nthis sample had lower levels of education…than the men，说女性教育水平低于男性，\n等于反过来说男性教育水平高于女性。问 men 的题，答案藏在'拿 women 跟 men 比'的这\n句里，需做一次方向反推。\n【替换】Women in this sample had lower levels of education…than the men ↔\nThey had more education than their spouses\n'女低于男'与'男多于女'肯否方向对调、等值\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 lower occupational achievements 里确有 achievements 一词，但它是用来说女\n性'职业成就低'的，并没说男性'从事业成就中获益良多'。选项 benefited a lot from their\ncareer achievements 借 achievements 设套，且把描述对象错安到'获益'这一原文没有的\n意思上。\n借用原文：lower occupational achievements\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里与 occupation 沾边的只有 occupational achievements，原文从未说男性'对配偶\n的职业感兴趣'。选项 showed interest in their spouse's occupations 用 occupations 这\n个原词凑出一个原文没有的行为。\n借用原文：lower occupational achievements\nC) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰在讲男女'教育与职业成就有差距'（女性更低），而 They had much in common\nwith their spouses 说男性'与配偶有很多共同点'，方向正好相反，原文也根本没提共同\n点。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What is said to be a key challenge for cities prone to floods?", "options": ["A) Eliminating their root cause.", "B) Forecasting flood risks accurately.", "C) Identifying the cities' geological features.", "D) Finding out the changing climate patterns."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Forecasting flood risks accurately.\n【定位】So understanding and accurately forecasting flood risks is a key challenge for vulnerable\ncities around the globe.\n因此，理解并准确预报洪水风险，对全球易受灾的城市来说是一项关键挑战。\n【信号】原句直接出现 key challenge。这句直接含题干核心名词 a key challenge for vulnerable\ncities around the globe，且前面有 So 引出结论，是明确的答案句信号——题干名词在录\n音里几乎原样出现的那一句，就是落点。\n【替换】accurately forecasting flood risks ↔ Forecasting flood risks accurately\n同词换序，意思全等\n【排除】A) 未提及\nEliminating their root cause 谈\"消除洪水的根源\"，录音通篇讲的是\"理解和预报\"风险，从\n未提到消除根源，属凭常识臆想的方向。\nC) 答非所问\nIdentifying the cities' geological features 说挑战是\"识别城市地质特征\"，录音里\nchallenge 后面跟的是\"预报洪水风险\"，根本没提地质特征，方向被岔开。\nD) 原词陷阱\nFinding out the changing climate patterns 借了录音开头 Climate scientists predict 里\n的 climate 制造熟耳感，但那只是引出背景的\"气候科学家预测\"，挑战并非\"弄清气候模式变\n化\"，是典型听到原词就想选的陷阱。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "For what purpose does Derek Loftis collect the data of ordinary citizens during high tides?", "options": ["A) To validate his hypothesis about the gravity of floods.", "B) To determine the frequency of high tides causing floods.", "C) To improve his mathematical flooding model.", "D) To see the feasibility of his project on flooding."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） To improve his mathematical flooding model.\n【定位】He'll use those data to validate and improve his mathematical flooding model,\ncalled TideWatch.\n他将用这些数据去验证并改进他那个名为 TideWatch 的数学洪水模型。\n【信号】use those data to 引出目的。这句的 use those data to validate and improve his\nmathematical flooding model 正是目的信号 use… to…，to 后面的动作就是采集数据的\n目的，是答案句无疑。\n【替换】improve his mathematical flooding model ↔ To improve his mathematical flooding model\n几乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo validate his hypothesis about the gravity of floods 借了录音原词 validate and\nimprove，但录音 validate 的宾语是\"数学洪水模型\"，不是\"关于洪水严重性的假设\"——盗\n了动词、换了宾语。\nB) 原词陷阱\nTo determine the frequency of high tides causing floods 盗用 high tides，但录音里\nhigh tides 是\"采集数据的时机\"，目的并非\"测高潮致洪的频率\"，把时间状语硬说成目的。\nD) 未提及\nTo see the feasibility of his project on flooding 说目的是\"看项目可行性\"，录音从未提\nfeasibility 或\"看可行性\"，纯属添加。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the passage say is Derek Loftis's mission?", "options": ["A) To study the consequences of high tides on flooded areas.", "B) To teach local citizens how to collect data of incoming floods.", "C) To forecast rapid floods in real time.", "D) To classify the flooding data processed."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To forecast rapid floods in real time.\n【定位】Loftis's mission is to know where the water goes before it goes there. ... But they still didn't\nhave the critically important ability to forecast fast-moving floods in real time.\nLoftis 的使命是：在水流到某处之前，就知道它会去哪里。……但他们仍不具备实时预报快\n速移动洪水这一至关重要的能力。\n【信号】mission is to + 后文同义解释。信号句 Loftis's mission is to know where the water\ngoes before it goes there 直接点出使命；它较抽象，录音随后用 forecast fast-moving\nfloods in real time 把这一使命具体化，两处合起来锁定答案。\n【替换】to know where the water goes before it goes there ↔ To forecast rapid floods in real time\n形象说法换成专业说法\nforecast fast-moving floods in real time ↔ To forecast rapid floods in real time\nfast-moving=rapid，近义词替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo study the consequences of high tides on flooded areas 借了录音的 high tides，但\n录音说的使命是\"实时预报\"，不是\"研究高潮对淹没区的后果\"，study consequences 是录\n音没有的动作。\nB) 张冠李戴\nTo teach local citizens how to collect data of incoming floods 盗用 citizens、collect\ndata，但录音是\"市民帮他采数据\"，从没说他的使命是\"教市民怎么采数据\"——角色与动作\n都被偷换。\nD) 原词陷阱\nTo classify the flooding data processed 借了录音 processing that information 里的\"处\n理数据\"概念，但录音从没说使命是\"给处理过的洪水数据分类\"，classify 是凭空添加的动\n作。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What did Loftis and his colleagues do to obtain data about water height and movement?", "options": ["A) They set up Internet-connected water-level sensors.", "B) They tracked the rising tides with video-cameras.", "C) They used newly-developed supercomputing facilities.", "D) They observed the direction of water flow on the spot."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They set up Internet-connected water-level sensors.\n【定位】So, in 2017, Loftis and colleagues set up a group of 28 Internet-connected water-level sensors\nthroughout the flood risk community of Norfolk, Virginia. The new sensors relayed rough\nmeasurements about water height and movement to a computer at the Virginia Institute.\n于是，2017 年，Loftis 和同事们在弗吉尼亚州诺福克的涝灾社区各处架设了一组共 28 个联\n网水位传感器。这些新传感器把关于水位高度和水流移动的粗略测量数据传回弗吉尼亚研究\n所的一台计算机。\n【信号】set up sensors + 紧跟 water height and movement。答案句信号是动作动词 set up a\ngroup of 28 Internet-connected water-level sensors，紧接着下一句出现题干的对象词\nwater height and movement，两句锁死\"靠装传感器拿水位数据\"。\n【替换】set up a group of 28 Internet-connected water-level sensors ↔\nThey set up Internet-connected water-level sensors\n略去数量，核心动作器材全留\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nThey tracked the rising tides with video-cameras 看似对应录音 take photos of rising\ntides，但那是\"市民用（手机）拍照\"，不是\"用 video-cameras 追踪\"，且这是采图片、不\n是题干问的\"水位与水流\"数据，器材和数据类型都被换。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey used newly-developed supercomputing facilities 借了录音 supercomputing had\nadvanced，但录音说超算是用来\"整合天气预报模型\"的背景能力，不是他们获取水位数据\n的手段，原词被搬错了位置。\nD) 未提及\nThey observed the direction of water flow on the spot 说他们\"实地观察水流方向\"，录\n音获取水流靠的是传感器测量，从没说\"实地肉眼观察方向\"，属添加动作。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say has become something of a ritual?", "options": ["A) To argue about the value of a college degree.", "B) To debate the validity of current survey data.", "C) To account for the drastic decline in employment among men.", "D) To compare men without college degrees with those who have."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To argue about the value of a college degree.\n【定位】Now, it's become something of a ritual to debate the value of a college degree, but as Current\nPopulation Survey data show, there has been a sharp decline in employment among men\nwithout college degrees compared with those who have them.\n如今，争论大学学历的价值已经几乎成了一种惯例，但正如\"当前人口调查\"的数据所显示\n的，没有大学学历的男性其就业出现了急剧下降，与有学历的人相比尤其如此。\n【信号】标志词 ritual 紧跟其定义。答案句里 it's become something of a ritual to debate the\nvalue of a college degree 中 ritual 后面紧接的 to debate… 就是\"惯例\"的具体内容，标志\n词后面紧跟的不定式就是要听的落点。\n【替换】ritual to debate the value of a college degree ↔ To argue about the value of a college degree.\ndebate 同义替换为 argue about\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里确有 Current Population Survey data show，B 项 To debate the validity of\ncurrent survey data. 借走 survey、data 让你\"听到就想选\"，但原文是\"数据显示\"，并\n非\"争论数据是否可信\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：Current Population Survey data show\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 项 To account for the drastic decline in employment among men. 搬来了答案句后半\n的 a sharp decline in employment among men without college degrees，但\"成为惯\n例的\"是前半句\"争论学历价值\"，\"解释就业下降\"是全篇要做的事、不是被称作 ritual 的那件\n事，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a sharp decline in employment among men without college degrees\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 To compare men without college degrees with those who have. 用了 men\nwithout college degrees compared with those who have them 的字眼，但 compared\n在原文只是描述\"下降\"的对照背景，说话人意图并非\"做对比\"这件事本身，过度解读。\n借用原文：men without college degrees compared with those who have them", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What did the researchers of a recent working paper consider first?", "options": ["A) The increase in women taking up jobs.", "B) The issue of changing job requirements.", "C) The factor of wages.", "D) The impact of inflation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The factor of wages.\n【定位】Let's look at that paper now. First, the researchers considered the issue of wages.\n现在我们来看这篇论文。首先，研究者们考虑了工资这一问题。\n【信号】次序信号词 First 直接领出答案。First, the researchers considered the issue of wages.\n里 First 就是\"最先考虑\"的信号，它后面紧跟的 the issue of wages 即答案落点。\n【替换】the researchers considered the issue of wages ↔ The factor of wages.\nthe issue of wages 概括为 The factor of wages\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 The increase in women taking up jobs. 对应录音 the increase in women\nparticipating in the workforce，但那是第 5 段\"新增解释\"里的内容，不是\"首先考虑\"的，\n答非所问。\n借用原文：the increase in women participating in the workforce\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项 The issue of changing job requirements. 指向 more jobs require a college\ndegree today than in the past，但录音明说这是 the second theory，不是\"首先\"考虑\n的，次序错位。\n借用原文：This leads to the second theory, that more jobs require a college degree today\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 The impact of inflation. 借走录音 adjusting for inflation 里的 inflation，但\ninflation 在原文只是\"调整通胀后\"的限定语，研究者首先考虑的是 wages，不是通胀，原\n词陷阱。\n借用原文：adjusting for inflation", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What did the recent paper identify as a new potential explanation of the problem concerning men's employment?", "options": ["A) Men's unwillingness to accept low wages in times of growing inflation.", "B) The wage gap between those with college degrees and those without.", "C) More jobs requiring their holders to have a college degree nowadays.", "D) The sharp decline in marriage among men with no college degrees."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The sharp decline in marriage among men with no college degrees.\n【定位】They identified two new potential explanations: the first being the tremendous decline in\nmarriage among men without college degrees since the 1960s; and the second being the\nincrease in women participating in the workforce that was concurrent with the decline\nin marriage.\n他们指出了两个新的可能解释：第一个是自上世纪六十年代以来，没有大学学历的男性婚姻\n率的剧烈下降；第二个是与婚姻下降同时出现的女性进入劳动力市场的增加。\n【信号】信号词 new、identified 领出两个新解释。They identified two new potential\nexplanations 里 new、identified 就是\"新提出\"的信号，冒号后的 the tremendous\ndecline in marriage among men without college degrees 即新解释之一。\n【替换】the tremendous decline in marriage among men without college degrees ↔\nThe sharp decline in marriage among men with no college degrees.\ntremendous=sharp、without=with no 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 Men's unwillingness to accept low wages in times of growing inflation. 拼接了\nwhy fewer men seem willing to accept those wages 与 inflation，但这是第一个旧理论\n的内容，不是\"新\"解释，且原文说工资经通胀调整后\"变化不大\"，并非\"通胀加剧\"，既答非\n所问又与原文相反。\n借用原文：why fewer men seem willing to accept those wages\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项 The wage gap between those with college degrees and those without. 几乎照抄\n录音 the wage gap has grown between those with college degrees and those\nwithout，但那是第 3 段已有的工资理论，不是第 5 段\"新提出\"的，原词陷阱。\n借用原文：As the wage gap has grown between those with college degrees and those without\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 More jobs requiring their holders to have a college degree nowadays. 对应\nmore jobs require a college degree today than in the past，但这是 the second\ntheory（旧理论），不在\"new potential explanations\"之列，答非所问。\n借用原文：more jobs require a college degree today than in the past", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What has a market research team found in its latest fashion and sustainability report?", "options": ["A) More and more people attach importance to protecting endangered animals.", "B) More and more people prioritize animal welfare when buying things to wear.", "C) An increasing number of people demand to free animals being kept in cages.", "D) An increasing number of people follow the latest trend of becoming vegetarians."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） More and more people prioritize animal welfare when buying things to wear.\n【定位】It found animal welfare came top of a list of issues that people considered before buying\nclothes, with 42% saying it was important to them.\n调查发现，在人们买衣服前会考虑的诸多问题中，动物福利排在首位，有 42% 的人表示这\n点对他们很重要。\n【信号】主体 It found 直接领出发现。录音用 It found animal welfare came top of a list of\nissues that people considered before buying clothes 把调查主体的「发现」直接端出\n来，「It found」就是答案句的信号——题目问发现什么，跟着 found 后面那句走即可。\n【替换】animal welfare came top of a list of issues that people considered before buying clothes ↔\nprioritize animal welfare when buying things to wear\ncame top=prioritize、buying clothes=things to wear\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音通篇讲 animal welfare(动物福利)和 animal-free(无动物成分)产品，从没说\nprotecting endangered animals(保护濒危动物)，A 把「动物福利」偷换成「濒危动物保\n护」，是听到 animal 就想选的诱饵。\nC) 未提及\n录音只字未提 free animals being kept in cages(放生笼中动物)，这是凭「动物」话题联\n想出来的内容，原文无据。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音开头确有 an increasing number of people are trying to eat less meat，但那只是引\n出话题的背景，不是调查「发现」的内容；D becoming vegetarians 盗用了开头的「吃\n肉/吃素」原词来答非所问。\n借用原文：While an increasing number of people are trying to eat less meat", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What have some world-renowned fashion designers done for some time?", "options": ["A) Avoided the use of leather and fur.", "B) Labelled all their products as vegan.", "C) Refrained from using chemicals in their products.", "D) Utilized a silk substitute made from mushrooms."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Avoided the use of leather and fur.\n【定位】At the top end, some of the world-renowned fashion designers have avoided leather and fur\nfor some time.\n在高端领域，一些世界知名的时装设计师已经有一段时间不用皮革和皮草了。\n【信号】主体词+for some time 原样复现。录音 some of the world-renowned fashion\ndesigners have avoided leather and fur for some time 把题干的主体词和 for some\ntime 几乎原样念出，这种「题干主体词在原文复现」就是答案句信号，紧跟其后的动词\navoided 即所做之事。\n【替换】have avoided leather and fur for some time ↔ Avoided the use of leather and fur.\n近乎原词、仅补 the use of\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音里 vegan 标签是 products registered with the vegan trademark、由 The Animal\nFree Product Society 与 New Look 等做的，不是高端设计师；B Labelled all their\nproducts as vegan. 把别人做的事安到设计师头上，且加了原文没有的 all。\n借用原文：products registered with the vegan trademark\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 the chemicals used for finishing them 是说做 vegan 产品要检查的环节，并未\n说设计师「拒用化学品」；C Refrained from using chemicals in their products. 盗用\nchemicals 原词却编造了「拒用」这个原文没有的动作。\n借用原文：the chemicals used for finishing them\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说 some of the companies are now exploring a leather substitute and a silk\nreplacement made from mushrooms，主体是 some companies、动作是 now\nexploring(正在探索)，既非「设计师长期所做」也非已经 utilized；D Utilized a silk\nsubstitute made from mushrooms. 盗用了 mushrooms 这个抢眼原词，但主体和时态都\n错位。\n借用原文：some of the companies are now exploring a leather substitute and a silk replacement\nmade from mushrooms", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker say is unclear concerning vegan products?", "options": ["A) Whether they actually signify a substantial change.", "B) Whether they effectively protect animals at large.", "C) Whether they can be considered sustainable.", "D) Whether they can be regarded as ethical."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Whether they can be regarded as ethical.\n【定位】However, it is unclear whether these new products can be considered ethical.\n然而，这些新产品是否能被视为合乎道德/伦理，尚不明确。\n【信号】However 转折后接 unclear。录音先说 This signifies a change，紧接 However, it is\nunclear whether these new products can be considered ethical——转折词 However\n加存疑词 unclear 双重信号叠加，转折之后才是真意，存疑的对象 ethical 就在这句里。\n【替换】whether these new products can be considered ethical ↔\nWhether they can be regarded as ethical.\nconsidered=regarded as 同义\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说 This signifies a change——「这标志着一种改变」是肯定语气、已成定论，恰恰不\n是 unclear 的对象；A a substantial change 盗用了 change 原词，却把已确定的事说成尚\n不明确，正话反说。\n借用原文：This signifies a change\nB) 答非所问\n录音整体讲 animal-free/animal welfare，但说 unclear 的那句问的是 ethical，不是\nprotect animals at large；B 借了全篇的「动物」话题来冒充存疑对象，原文没把「是否真\n护动物」列为不明确之事。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音只在开头报告名 fashion and sustainability report 出现过 sustainability，从未说\n「是否可持续」尚不明确；C sustainable 盗用了这个开头出现的原词来答非所问。\n借用原文：fashion and sustainability report", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What is one of the facts stated by the speaker?", "options": ["A) The era we live in is the most peaceful in history.", "B) The world has seen more violence in recent years.", "C) The environmental welfare has worsened in the world.", "D) The belief is less prevalent that the world is going to hell."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The era we live in is the most peaceful in history.\n【定位】We are living in history's most peaceful era, with violence of all kinds in steep decline. Those\nare the facts.\n我们正生活在历史上最和平的时代，各类暴力都在急剧下降。这些就是事实。\n【信号】'Those are the facts' 直接点明这是事实。演讲者紧接着用 Those are the facts. 给前面一\n串陈述盖章为'事实'，这就是答案信号；其中 history's most peaceful era 正对应题干要的\n那个 fact。\n【替换】history's most peaceful era ↔ the most peaceful in history\n同义改写：词序调换、词性微调\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n选项说 more violence in recent years，但录音明说 violence of all kinds in steep\ndecline（各类暴力急剧下降），方向正好相反，是把'下降'听反成'增多'。\n借用原文：violence of all kinds in steep decline\nC) 与原文相反\n选项称 environmental welfare has worsened，但录音说每一项 environmental\nwelfare…has improved rather than deteriorated（改善而非恶化），用 worsened 偷换\n了 improved，方向反了。\n借用原文：Every single measure of material and environmental welfare…has improved rather\nthan deteriorated\nD) 未提及\n选项说'认为世界要完蛋的信念不那么流行了'，但录音恰恰描述这种悲观信念很普遍\n（everything was terrible, and that the world was going to hell、第 3 段多数英法人觉\n得世界在变坏），从没说它在减弱，属凭空生造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do we learn about most British and French people in a 2015 survey for YouGov?", "options": ["A) They were actually not in their right mind.", "B) They believed the world was deteriorating.", "C) They did not wish to live in the previous century.", "D) They were convinced by the statistics presented to them."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They believed the world was deteriorating.\n【定位】In a 2015 survey for YouGov, 65% of British people and 81% of the French said they thought\nthe world was getting worse.\n在 2015 年一项 YouGov 的调查中，65% 的英国人和 81% 的法国人说他们认为世界正在\n变糟。\n【信号】专名 YouGov/2015 直接领出答案句。题干的专名锚点 In a 2015 survey for YouGov 在\n原文原样出现，紧跟其后的 said they thought the world was getting worse 就是这群人\n的看法，即答案信号。\n【替换】the world was getting worse ↔ the world was deteriorating\n同义改写：getting worse = deteriorating\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项说这群人 not in their right mind，原文确有 Nobody in their right mind should\nwish to have lived in a previous century，但这是演讲者自己的评论，不是调查显示这群\n人神志不清；把演讲者的话安到调查对象头上。\n借用原文：Nobody in their right mind should wish to have lived in a previous century.\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 did not wish to live in the previous century 同样取自 Nobody in their right mind\nshould wish to have lived in a previous century，但这仍是演讲者的论断，且原文是\n说'没人该希望活在过去'，并非调查里这群人表达了此意愿。\n借用原文：Nobody in their right mind should wish to have lived in a previous century.\nD) 与原文相反\n选项说他们 convinced by the statistics presented to them，但原文说按 numerous\nsensible statistics, these people are simply wrong——统计数据恰恰证明他们错了，他\n们并未被数据说服，方向相反。\n借用原文：according to numerous sensible statistics, these people are simply wrong", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does our prevailing mood of despair indicate?", "options": ["A) The subjectivity of mass media.", "B) The current state of affairs.", "C) Our psychological biases.", "D) Our ancestors' influence."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Our psychological biases.\n【定位】It is best explained as the result of various psychological biases that served a purpose for our\nevolutionary ancestors—but now, in a media-saturated era, constantly mislead us.\n它最好被解释为种种心理偏见的结果——这些偏见曾对我们的进化祖先有用，但如今在一个\n被媒体充斥的时代，却不断误导我们。\n【信号】归因信号 'explained as the result of'。演讲者用 It is best explained as the result of\nvarious psychological biases 明确把这股绝望情绪归因到'心理偏见'，explained as the\nresult of 就是答案信号，其后名词即题目要的答案。\n【替换】various psychological biases ↔ Our psychological biases.\n近乎原词，仅限定词不同\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n选项说 The subjectivity of mass media.，原文虽提到 in a media-saturated era（媒体充\n斥的时代），但那只是偏见'被放大'的背景，演讲者归因的根源是心理偏见，而非'媒体的主\n观性'，把背景偷换成根源。\n借用原文：in a media-saturated era, constantly mislead us\nB) 与原文相反\n选项说反映 The current state of affairs.，但原文明说这情绪 says more about us than it\ndoes about how things really are——它说的是我们自己，而非事情的真实状况，正好相\n反。\n借用原文：It says more about us than it does about how things really are.\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Our ancestors' influence. 盗用原文 served a purpose for our evolutionary\nancestors 里的 ancestors，听到这个词很想选；但原文是说偏见曾对祖先有用，演讲者把\n绝望情绪直接归因到'心理偏见'本身，而非'祖先的影响'。\n借用原文：that served a purpose for our evolutionary ancestors", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What was a very good thing in our evolutionary past?", "options": ["A) Spreading exciting news around us far and wide.", "B) Vacuuming up depressing or enraging stories.", "C) Calculating dangerous risks to our survival.", "D) Paying attention to negative information."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Paying attention to negative information.\n【定位】In our evolutionary past, it was a very good thing that our attention could be easily seized by\nnegative information, since it might well indicate a dangerous risk to our own survival.\n在我们的进化过去，注意力容易被负面信息攫取是一件很好的事，因为这很可能意味着对我\n们自身生存的危险威胁。\n【信号】原句直给 'a very good thing that…'。题干 a very good thing 在原文原样出现于 it\nwas a very good thing that our attention could be easily seized by negative\ninformation，that 从句里的内容就是那件'好事'，即答案信号。\n【替换】our attention could be easily seized by negative information ↔\nPaying attention to negative information.\n被动转主动 + 概括：被攫取注意 = 关注负面信息\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项说 Spreading exciting news around us far and wide.，但原文说 Really good\nnews…can be a lot harder to spot（好消息其实更难被注意到），且演讲者夸的是关\n注'坏'消息有生存价值，不是传播好消息，方向相反。\n借用原文：Really good news…can be a lot harder to spot in a newspaper\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 Vacuuming up depressing or enraging stories. 几乎照搬原文 vacuum up\ndepressing or enraging stories from across the globe，听到原词极想选；但原文说这\n是'当今超连接时代'我们对坏消息上瘾的恶果，而非'进化过去的好事'，时间与褒贬都对不\n上。\n借用原文：our addiction to bad news just leads us to vacuum up depressing or enraging stories\nfrom across the globe\nC) 偷换概念\n选项说 Calculating dangerous risks to our survival.，原文有 a dangerous risk to our\nown survival，但说的是负面信息可能'意味着'生存危险，故关注它是好事，并未说去'计\n算'风险；把'感知威胁'偷换成'计算风险'。\n借用原文：it might well indicate a dangerous risk to our own survival", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) differentiate\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can + 动词原形\n【依据】differentiate between A and B 固定搭配\nhow we __(26)__ between statements expressing what is real versus what is merely\npossible，differentiate between 表示「在 A 与 B 之间作区分」，与上文 respond to\nlanguage expressing facts differently than they do to words conveying possibility 的\ndifferently 同根呼应。\n【竞争词】C) ascertain 不与 between 搭配\nascertain 义为「查明」，常接 that 从句或名词，不接 between，搭配上直接出局。\nH) inhibit 义为「抑制」与「区分」语义相反\ninhibit 表示阻止，与上下文「区分事实和可能」无关，且不接 between。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 can + 动词原形"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) deliberate\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 后接表语形容词\n【依据】与 deceptive advertisements 同向：蓄意欺骗\nSome of this is __(27)__, as is the case with deceptive advertisements, but the\nproblem is __(28)__ by individuals who believe they are sharing correct information.\nbut 之后是「自以为正确」的无意之失，那么 but 之前的虚假信息就是「故意为之」的；\ndeliberate「蓄意的」正与 deceptive 同义递进。\n【竞争词】N) unanimous 义为「全体一致」与虚假信息无关\nunanimous 修饰意见或决议的「一致同意」，无法描述 some of this（部分虚假信息），且与\ndeceptive 也无语义关联。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词 is 后接表语形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) aggravated\n动词\n【词性槽】be + 过去分词构成被动语态\n【依据】「问题被…加剧」与上下文反差逻辑契合\n上文 Some of this is __(27)__, as is the case with deceptive advertisements, but the\nproblem is __(28)__ by individuals who believe they are sharing correct\ninformation，but 之后引入「以为自己在分享正确信息的人」也让问题恶化——\naggravated「使加剧」精确表达「使问题更严重」。\n【竞争词】A) activated 义为「激活」与「问题恶化」语义不符\nactivate 多用于激活机制、开关、神经元；the problem is activated 不是地道表达，也不符合\nbut 转折所要强调的「问题更糟」语义。activated 留给 34 题更贴切（脑活动被激活）。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— activated 误填被动语态。the problem is __(28)__ by\nindividuals 被动语态卡位，A)activated 与 B)aggravated 都是过去分词；但「problem\n被激活」搭配反常，且 but 转折要求「程度加深」语义，应选 aggravated。防坑：被动语\n态先看搭配地道度，再看上下文转折方向。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be + 过去分词构成被动语态"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) speculative\n形容词\n【词性槽】or 并列两个形容词作宾语补足语\n【依据】与 possible 同义递进、扣题「可能性」\n全文核心对立是 fact vs possibility，此处用 or merely 把 possible 进一步弱化为「仅仅是\n推测性的」，speculative 正好表达「猜测性的、未经证实的」，与 the factual from the\npossible 完美对应。\n【竞争词】D) deliberate 义为「蓄意的」与上下文无关\ndeliberate 强调「故意的」，to separate the factual from the possible or merely __(29)__\n谈的是事实/可能/推测的区分，没有「故意」的语义场。\nN) unanimous 义为「全体一致」与单数抽象类别冲突\nunanimous 一般用于人群意见一致，无法与 possible 并列描述命题性质；且与\nfact/possibility 对立无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·or 并列两个形容词作宾语补足语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) evokes\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 + ___ + 宾语 一般现在时谓语\n【依据】evoke responses 是固定搭配且时态正确\ninformation presented as fact __(30)__ special responses in our brains，evoke\nresponses 表示「引起反应」是常见高频搭配；主句为一般现在时陈述规律，三单形态\nevokes 正好匹配主语 information。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 + ___ + 宾语 一般现在时谓"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) uncertainty\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 of 后接名词作宾语\n【依据】与「事实 vs 可能」对照中表示「不确定性」\n上文 information presented as fact __(30)__ special responses in our brains, which\nare distinct from when we process the same content with clear indicators of\n__(31)__，把 fact 与「带不确定性标记的同样内容」对照——uncertainty 恰好是 fact 的对\n立面，扣全文 fact vs possibility 主题。\n【竞争词】F) discourse 义为「话语」与「事实/可能」对照无关\nindicators of discourse「话语的指示物」不符合上下文要表达的「不确定性的标记」。\nI) manuscript 义为「手稿」属生僻名词且话题不沾\nmanuscript 与脑神经研究、可能性话题没有关联。\nK) remnants 义为「残余物」复数且语义偏离\nremnants 多指物质残余，indicators of remnants 不构成有意义短语，且与主题无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 of 后接名词作宾语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) ascertain\n动词\n【词性槽】不定式 to 后接动词原形\n【依据】「打算弄清楚大脑如何运算」语义契合\nascertain how... 表示「查明…如何…」，与科研动机 intended to 完美搭配；下句紧接\nThe researchers compared brain responses to statements expressing factual __(33)__\nand those expressing possibility. 正是「弄清」的具体做法。\n【竞争词】H) inhibit 义为「抑制」与研究目的相反\ninhibit 表示阻止脑的运算，科学家研究意图是「了解」而非「抑制」，语义反向。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定式 to 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) scenarios\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 factual 后接名词作宾语\n【依据】factual scenarios「事实性情景」与 possibility 并列\nscenarios 指「情景、设想」，与下文例句 \"There is a monster under my bed\"\nexemplifies a factual statement. 中举出的「场景陈述」对应；factual scenarios（事实性\n场景）与 statements expressing possibility（表可能性的陈述）正好对照。\n【竞争词】F) discourse 义为抽象「话语」与 possibility 不对仗\ndiscourse 偏「论述、话语」整体概念，无法与具体的 possibility（可能性）并列做比较对象。\nI) manuscript 义为「手稿」与科研脑反应话题无关\nfactual manuscript 不是常用搭配，且与「比较脑反应」的实验语境完全脱节。\nK) remnants 义为「残余」搭配不成立\nfactual remnants 不构成有意义短语，且 remnants 偏物质义，不能与 possibility 并列。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 factual 后接名词作宾语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) activated\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 + ___ + 宾语 缺谓语\n【依据】「事实性语言激活了脑活动激增」时态一致\n前后语境 the study showed that factual language __(34)__ a rapid increase in brain\nactivity，主句 showed 是过去式，从句保持时态一致用过去式 activated；且 activate\nbrain activity 是脑科学常用搭配。G)evokes 是一般现在时，会与 showed 产生时态不一\n致，且 30 题更需要它。\n【竞争词】G) evokes 时态不一致且义近但留给 30 题\nevokes 是三单现在时，与 the study showed 过去式语境不匹配；且 evoke special responses\n与 30 题的搭配更典型，应把 evokes 留给 30 题。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— evokes 时态混淆。the study showed that factual\nlanguage __(34)__ a rapid increase in brain activity 主句过去式 showed，宾语从句也应\n过去式 activated；很多考生不查时态、看见 evokes responses 搭配地道就抢填，结果把\n30 题的「唯一解」抢走。防坑：先看主句时态，再选词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 + ___ + 宾语 缺谓语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) activated", "B) aggravated", "C) ascertain", "D) deliberate", "E) differentiate", "F) discourse", "G) evokes", "H) inhibit", "I) manuscript", "J) marvels", "K) remnants", "L) scenarios", "M) speculative", "N) unanimous", "O) uncertainty"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) discourse\n名词\n【词性槽】现在分词 processing 后接名词宾语\n【依据】「处理话语」呼应全文「语言加工」主题\n全文一直在讨论大脑对**语言**的加工 Our brains respond to language expressing facts\ndifferently than they do to words conveying possibility；Brain regions involved in\nprocessing discourse rapidly distinguish facts from possibilities「参与话语加工的脑区\n能迅速区分事实与可能」恰好把「大脑加工语言」这条线收束起来。\n【竞争词】I) manuscript 义为「手稿」具体且与脑加工无关\nmanuscript 指实体手稿，processing manuscript 不构成有意义的脑科学短语，且与全文谈论\n「语言/话语」脱节。\nJ) marvels 义为「奇迹」与话题无关\nprocessing marvels 没有学术含义，且全文从未涉及「奇迹」概念。\nK) remnants 义为「残余」搭配与话题双不匹配\nprocessing remnants「处理残余物」不是脑科学常见表达，且与「区分事实与可能」无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·现在分词 processing 后接名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Exploration of shipwrecks on the sea floor is crucial in updating our understanding of humanity's past.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】新认知 + 人类过去\n【定位】Some—like an ancient Roman ship found off Antikythera, Greece, dated between 70 and 60\nBC and carrying astonishingly sophisticated gears and dials for navigating by the sun—are\ncritical to a new understanding of our past.\n一些沉船——比如在希腊安提基特拉海域发现的、年代在公元前 70 至 60 年之间、装载着\n精巧得惊人的太阳导航齿轮与刻度盘的古罗马船——对我们重新理解过去至关重要。\n【改写】Exploration of shipwrecks on the sea floor is crucial ↔\nare critical to a new understanding of our past\ncrucial 与 critical 同义\nupdating our understanding of humanity's past ↔ a new understanding of our past\n动词名词化对应\n【辨邻】 C technology can reveal the precious artifacts 讲的是技术能 ‘显形’ 海底文物，与\nupdating our understanding of humanity's past 强调的 ‘刷新对过去的认知’ 不在同\n一落点。\nadvances in radar, diving, detection equipment, computers, and GPS have transformed the hunt\nB dozens of majestic Old World sailing ships smashed and sank 只交代历史上确有\n沉船，并未谈这些沉船的探查 “刷新了对过去的理解”。\ndozens of majestic Old World sailing ships smashed and sank on this irregular stretch of windy\nFlorida coast", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Quite a number of majestic ships sailing from Europe to America were wrecked off the Florida coast over the centuries.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】数艘欧洲船 + 佛州海岸沉没\n【定位】Over the centuries, dozens of majestic Old World sailing ships smashed and sank on this\nirregular stretch of windy Florida coast.\n几个世纪以来，数十艘庄严的旧大陆远洋帆船在这段不规则的多风弗罗里达海岸触礁沉没。\n【改写】Quite a number of majestic ships ↔ dozens of majestic Old World sailing ships\n数量+majestic 同义复现\nsailing from Europe to America ↔ Old World sailing ships\nOld World 上位指欧洲\nwere wrecked off the Florida coast ↔\nsmashed and sank on this irregular stretch of windy Florida coast\nwrecked 同义于 smashed and sank\nover the centuries ↔ Over the centuries\n时间状语原样复现\n【辨邻】 A Nearly 1.5 million visitors flock here every year 写的是游客与航天，没有任何\n‘majestic ships 沉没’ 的事实。\nNearly 1.5 million visitors flock here every year to watch rockets, spacecraft, and satellites blast off\nG he had discovered scattered remains of a ship buried a kilometer off Cape\nCanaveral 聚焦单船发现，与 Quite a number of majestic ships 这一群体性量化表\n述对不上。\nhe had discovered scattered remains of a ship buried a kilometer off Cape Canaveral", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Pritchett suffered a heavy loss when a US district court ruled against him.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Pritchett + 美地区法院败诉\n【定位】In 2018, two long years after Pritchett's discovery, the federal district court ruled in favor of\nFrance. For Pritchett, the decision was devastating. Millions of dollars of investor funding\nand years of labor were lost.\n2018 年，在 Pritchett 发现沉船两年后，联邦地区法院判决支持法国。对 Pritchett 而言，\n这一决定是毁灭性的；数百万美元的投资资金和多年的劳动付之东流。\n【改写】Pritchett suffered a heavy loss ↔\nMillions of dollars of investor funding and years of labor were lost\nheavy loss 同义于 millions+years lost\na US district court ruled against him ↔ the federal district court ruled in favor of France\n支持对方=判本人败\n【辨邻】 J Spain won a five-year legal battle against Odyssey Marine Exploration 讲的是\nOdyssey 公司败诉，不是 Pritchett。\nSpain won a five-year legal battle against Odyssey Marine Exploration\nH The discovery was cause for celebration 是正面发现事件，与 suffered a heavy\nloss 完全相反。\nThe discovery was cause for celebration", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Recently, people who found treasures in shipwrecks have been sued over their rights to own them.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】最近 + 找到宝藏者被诉所有权\n【定位】But of late, when salvors have found vessels, their rights have been challenged in court.\n但近来，当打捞人找到沉船时，他们的权利在法庭上遭到挑战。\n【改写】Recently ↔ of late\n时间副词同义\npeople who found treasures in shipwrecks ↔ when salvors have found vessels\nsalvors 上位为 people\nhave been sued over their rights to own them ↔ their rights have been challenged in court\nsued=challenged in court\n【辨邻】 I And then the legal storm began, with GME and Pritchett pitted against Florida\nand France 只讲 Pritchett 这一桩官司，没有 people who found treasures 暗含的\n群体性。\nAnd then the legal storm began, with GME and Pritchett pitted against Florida and France\nJ But this is far from the first time a salvor has lost all rights to a discovery 强调\n‘不是第一次’，是补充例证，与陈述句强调的 ‘Recently 出现这种官司’ 的时间锚点不\n吻合。\nBut this is far from the first time a salvor has lost all rights to a discovery", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Pritchett claims he got support of millions of dollars from investors for his shipwreck exploration.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Pritchett + 数百万美元投资\n【定位】Over the prior three years, he and his crew had obtained 14 state permits to survey a nearly\n260-square-kilometer area off the cape; they worked 250 days a year, backed by investor\nfunds of, he claims, US $4 million.\n在此前三年中，他和团队取得 14 张州许可，勘测卡角外近 260 平方公里区域；他们每年工\n作 250 天，他自称获得 400 万美元投资资金支持。\n【改写】Pritchett claims ↔ he claims, US $4 million\nclaims 原样复现且对应主语\nmillions of dollars from investors ↔ investor funds of, he claims, US $4 million\n$4 million 即 millions 量级\nfor his shipwreck exploration ↔ to survey a nearly 260-square-kilometer area off the cape\nsurvey=exploration\n【辨邻】 I Millions of dollars of investor funding and years of labor were lost 讲的是钱亏掉\n了，与 got support of millions of dollars 的 ‘得到支持’ 方向相反。\nMillions of dollars of investor funding and years of labor were lost\nH The artifacts indicated the divers had likely found the wreck of La Trinité 只讲发\n现物，没有任何投资人或金额信息。\nThe artifacts indicated the divers had likely found the wreck of La Trinité", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "One pioneer marine scientist thinks archaeologists should make greater efforts to publish their findings.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】先驱海洋学者 + 多发表\n【定位】\"Archaeology has a terrible reputation for not publishing enough on its excavations and\nfinds,\" he says.\n“考古学有一个糟糕的名声，就是不充分发表其发掘与发现的成果，” 他说。\n【改写】One pioneer marine scientist ↔ George Bass is one of the pioneers of under-water archaeology\nmarine scientist 上位概括\nshould make greater efforts to publish their findings ↔\narchaeologists need to do a better job themselves instead of routinely criticizing treasure\nhunters\nshould=need to do a better job\npublish their findings ↔ not publishing enough on its excavations and finds\npublish their findings 同源\n【辨邻】 K Putting an inflated price on artifacts rather than viewing them as cultural and\nhistorical treasures 与发表问题无关，也没有 pioneer 这一身份词。\nPutting an inflated price on artifacts rather than viewing them as cultural and historical treasures\nthat transcend any price is what irritates many archaeologists", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "With technological advancement in recent years, salvors now can detect the invaluable man-made objects lying buried under the sea.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】技术进步 + 探测海底人工物\n【定位】In recent years, advances in radar, diving, detection equipment, computers, and GPS have\ntransformed the hunt. The naked eye might see a pile of rocks, but technology can reveal the\nprecious artifacts that lie hidden on the ocean floor.\n近年来，雷达、潜水、探测设备、计算机与 GPS 的进步已彻底改变了搜寻方式。肉眼可能\n只看见一堆岩石，但技术能揭示藏匿在海底的珍贵人工制品。\n【改写】With technological advancement in recent years ↔\nIn recent years, advances in radar, diving, detection equipment, computers, and GPS\n时间状语+概括-列表对应\nsalvors now can detect the invaluable man-made objects ↔\ntechnology can reveal the precious artifacts\ndetect=reveal, invaluable=precious, man-made objects=artifacts\nlying buried under the sea ↔ lie hidden on the ocean floor\nburied=hidden, under the sea=on the ocean floor\n【辨邻】 G Using computer technology, Pritchett and his crew created intricate, color-\ncoded maps 是 Pritchett 团队的具体操作，与陈述句要的 ‘普遍 salvors 能 detect 文\n物’ 的总论性表述不一致。\nUsing computer technology, Pritchett and his crew created intricate, color-coded maps marked with\nthe GPS coordinates of thousands of finds\nH the magnetometer picked up metal that turned out to be an iron cannon 聚焦\n于一次具体的探测结果，覆盖不到陈述句的总论性范围。\nOne day in 2015, the magnetometer picked up metal that turned out to be an iron cannon", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "According to a lawyer, many treasure hunters are susceptible to loss because they are unaware they face a financially stronger opponent in court.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】律师 + 对手财力雄厚\n【定位】\"Many treasure hunters don't understand they are going to have to fight for their rights against\na government that has an endless supply of money for legal battles that treasure hunters are\nlikely to lose.\"\n“许多寻宝者不明白，他们要与一个拥有无尽资金打官司的政府争夺权利，而寻宝者很可能\n会输。”\n【改写】According to a lawyer ↔ says attorney David Concannon\nlawyer=attorney\nmany treasure hunters are susceptible to loss ↔ treasure hunters are likely to lose\nsusceptible to loss=likely to lose\nthey are unaware they face a financially stronger opponent in court ↔\nMany treasure hunters don't understand they are going to have to fight for their rights against\na government that has an endless supply of money for legal battles\nunaware=don't understand, financially stronger=endless supply of money\n【辨邻】 I In 2018, two long years after Pritchett's discovery, the federal district court\nruled in favor of France 是法院判决叙事，没有律师作为信息来源。\nIn 2018, two long years after Pritchett's discovery, the federal district court ruled in favor of France\nE opposing, and equally contemptuous, tribes 强调阵营对立，没有 ‘财力悬殊导致打\n捞人易输’ 这一核心命题。\nthey belong to opposing, and equally contemptuous, tribes", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Salvors of treasures in sunken ships and marine archaeologists are now hostile to each other.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】打捞人与考古学家敌对\n【定位】Where once salvors and archaeologists worked side by side, now they belong to opposing,\nand equally contemptuous, tribes.\n曾经打捞人和考古学家肩并肩工作，如今他们分属对立的、互相轻蔑的两个阵营。\n【改写】Salvors of treasures in sunken ships and marine archaeologists ↔ salvors and archaeologists\n群体名一一对应\nare now hostile to each other ↔ now they belong to opposing, and equally contemptuous, tribes\nopposing+contemptuous=hostile\n【辨邻】 D salvors and their investors—as well as marine archaeologists wanting to bring\nto light the lost relics 只是并列罗列两类人，未对两者关系作 ‘敌对’ 这一判断。\nfresh tide of salvors and their investors—as well as marine archaeologists wanting to bring to light\nthe lost relics\nK is what irritates many archaeologists 描述考古学家被某种做法激怒，是单向态度，\n与陈述句要的 ‘双方相互敌对’ 不一致。\nis what irritates many archaeologists", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Archaeologists want to see artifacts help humans understand their past instead of being sold to private collectors at an outrageous price.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】考古反对天价卖私人收藏\n【定位】Putting an inflated price on artifacts rather than viewing them as cultural and historical\ntreasures that transcend any price is what irritates many archaeologists. For the archaeologist,\neverything in a wreck matters—hair, fabric, a fragment of a newspaper, rat bones-all things\nspeak volumes. Archaeologists don't want artifacts ending up in a private collection instead of\ntaking humanity on a journey of understanding.\n把文物标上虚高的价格，而不是把它们看作超越任何价格的文化与历史财富，是令许多考古\n学家恼火的事。对考古学家来说，沉船里的一切都意义重大——头发、织物、报纸残片、\n老鼠骨——一切都饱含信息。考古学家不愿文物落入私人收藏，而非带领人类踏上一段理解\n之旅。\n【改写】Archaeologists want to see artifacts help humans understand their past ↔\ntaking humanity on a journey of understanding\nhelp humans understand 同义于 journey of understanding\ninstead of being sold to private collectors ↔ artifacts ending up in a private collection\nsold to private collectors=end up in private collection\nat an outrageous price ↔ Putting an inflated price on artifacts\noutrageous=inflated\n【辨邻】 E Archaeologists regard themselves as protectors of history, and they see salvors\nas careless destroyers 落到阵营冲突，没有 ‘private collectors / outrageous price’\n这两个具体落点。\nArchaeologists regard themselves as protectors of history, and they see salvors as\ncareless destroyers\nM But I also think I should get paid for what I found 是 Pritchett 的主张，与陈述句\n要的 ‘Archaeologists want …’ 主体相反。\nBut I also think I should get paid for what I found", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn about singer and actress Selena Gomez in the past four and a half years?", "options": ["A) She has had worsening mental problems.", "B) She has won Instagram's most followers.", "C) She has refrained from using social media.", "D) She has succeeded in a bid on the internet."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about singer and actress Selena Gomez in the past four and a half\nyears? 指向「她过去四年半发生了什么」这一具体事实，没有 infer/imply/mainly。\n【拆句】What do we learn about singer and actress Selena Gomez in the past four and a half\nyears? 是一般疑问句，主干 What do we learn；介词短语 in the past four and a half\nyears 作时间状语，限定考察范围。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Taking a break from social media was the best decision that I've ever made for my\nmental health\n远离社交媒体是我为心理健康做过的最好的决定。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说她远离社媒是为改善心理健康（the best decision that I've ever made for my\nmental health），而 She has had worsening mental problems 把「为改善心理健\n康」反过来说成「心理问题加重」，与原文方向相反。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说她「在 2016 年成为 Instagram 关注最多的用户」（she became Instagram's\nmost followed user in 2016），是过去某一时点的事实；She has won Instagram's\nmost followers 用现在完成时把这件 2016 年的事说成「过去四年半内取得」，与题干\nin the past four and a half years 不符。\nC) ✓ 正确\nTaking a break from social media was the best decision that I've ever made for my mental health\n↔ She has refrained from using social media\n预判「远离社媒」与 She has refrained from using social media 完全吻合，且原文-\n选项之间的替换属于六级阅读高频同义替换模式。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 in a bid to improve her mental health 中 bid 意为「努力、尝试」（熟词僻\n义），整个短语意为「为改善心理健康所作的努力」；She has succeeded in a bid on\nthe internet 把 bid 当作「竞拍」用，与原文意思完全两回事。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why does actively opting out of using the internet become a matter of privilege?", "options": ["A) Most people find it subtle and complicated to give up using the internet.", "B) Most people can hardly ditch the web while avoiding hate and comparisons.", "C) Most people can hardly get by without the internet due to growing digitisation.", "D) Most people have been seriously addicted to the web without being aware of it."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why does actively opting out of using the internet become a matter of privilege? 已\n把结论摆出，要在原文找直接给出的原因，属于原因型细节题。\n【拆句】Why does actively opting out of using the internet become a matter of privilege? 主\n干 does opting out become a matter of privilege；动名词短语 actively opting out of\nusing the internet 作主语；a matter of privilege 是表语。Why 直接问原因。\n【定位】The increasing digitisation of our society means that everything from paying a gas bill to\nplotting a route to a friend's house and even making a phone call is at the mercy of your\ninternet connection. Actively opting out of using the internet becomes a matter of privilege.\n我们社会日益数字化意味着——从交一笔煤气费、规划一条去朋友家的路线，甚至打一通电\n话——一切都受制于你的网络连接。主动选择不使用互联网由此变成一种特权。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nMost people find it subtle and complicated to give up using the internet 改写自\nDitching the web at large, however, is a far more subtle and complicated\nprospect，描述的是「难度」，并未回答题干「为何成为特权」。\nB) 张冠李戴\nMost people can hardly ditch the web while avoiding hate and comparisons 把\n「避开仇恨与攀比」塞给「为何不上网是特权」这一问题；但 The unnecessary hate\nand comparisons went away once I put my phone down 讲的是 Gomez 远离社媒\n后的结果，与 Q47 的因果无关。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThe increasing digitisation of our society means that everything from paying a gas bill to\nplotting a route to a friend's house and even making a phone call is at the mercy of your internet\nconnection\n↔ Most people can hardly get by without the internet due to growing digitisation\nCould you get by without using the internet for four and a half years? ↔\nMost people can hardly get by without the internet\n预判的因果（数字化 → 离不开网 → 不上网成特权）与 Most people can hardly get\nby without the internet due to growing digitisation 一一对应。\nD) 无中生有\nMost people have been seriously addicted to the web without being aware of it\n拿 like \"an addict\" 套到「普通人不自觉成瘾」上，但原文只说 Gomez 自己感觉像\naddict，并没有「普通人不自觉成瘾」这一论断，且与 Q47 因果无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why does the author say \"witnessing companies…feels particularly irritating\"(Lines 2-4, Para. 6)?", "options": ["A) The UK digital divide would further worsen due to the metaverse.", "B) The concept of the metaverse is believed to be still quite illusory.", "C) School children would be drawn farther away from the real world.", "D) Most families in the UK do not have stable broadband connections."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】Why does the author say \"witnessing companies…feels particularly irritating\" 问的是\n作者使用此评价语背后的逻辑，需要把「为何 irritating」这层因果挖出来。\n【拆句】Why does the author say \"witnessing companies…feels particularly irritating\"(Lines\n2-4, Para. 6)? Why 引导主干，宾语从句 the author say + 直接引语；括号 (Lines 2-\n4, Para. 6) 是定位说明。直接引语本身是动名词短语作主语 + feels 系动词 + irritating\n表语。\n【定位】The UK's digital divide has worsened over the past two years, leaving poorer families without\nbroadband connections in their homes. Digital exclusion is a major threat to wider societal\nequality in the UK, so witnessing companies like Facebook championing the metaverse(元宇\n宙) as the next great frontier when school children are struggling to complete their homework\nfeels particularly irritating.\n英国的数字鸿沟在过去两年里加剧，导致较贫困家庭家中没有宽带。数字排斥是英国社会整\n体公平的一大威胁，因此当学童正为完成作业犯难时，看到 Facebook 之类的公司还在把元\n宇宙鼓吹成下一个伟大前沿，就让人尤其感到刺目。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThe UK's digital divide has worsened over the past two years ↔\nThe UK digital divide would further worsen due to the metaverse\nchampioning the metaverse(元宇宙) as the next great frontier when school children are\nstruggling to complete their homework\n↔ due to the metaverse\n预判「鼓吹元宇宙会加深数字鸿沟」与 The UK digital divide would further worsen\ndue to the metaverse 完全对应，且推断只走一步，未越界。\nB) 无中生有\nThe concept of the metaverse is believed to be still quite illusory 评价的是「元宇\n宙是否虚幻」，原文只用 the next great frontier 描述公司的宣传口径，并未对\nmetaverse 概念本身的「虚幻与否」作判断。\nC) 答非所问\nSchool children would be drawn farther away from the real world 把焦点放在「学\n童被拉离现实世界」，但原文中学童的位置是 struggling to complete their\nhomework——他们是因为没有宽带连作业都做不了，而不是被元宇宙拉离现实。\nD) 以偏概全\nMost families in the UK do not have stable broadband connections 把原文\nleaving poorer families without broadband connections in their homes 的「较贫\n困家庭」扩大为「大多数家庭」；同时这只是引发 irritating 的背景之一，而不是作者觉\n得 irritating 的核心原因。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is worth bearing in mind concerning social media platforms?", "options": ["A) They are conducive to promoting societal equality.", "B) They help many people feel connected with others.", "C) They provide a necessary device for a digital detox.", "D) They create a virtual community on the internet."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is worth bearing in mind concerning social media platforms? 问的是作者明确提\n示要记住的事实，对应原文一处明确陈述。\n【拆句】What is worth bearing in mind concerning social media platforms? 主干 What is\nworth bearing in mind；动名词短语 bearing in mind 作 worth 的宾语；介词短语\nconcerning social media platforms 限定话题。结构清晰无陷阱。\n【定位】Consequently, it's worth bearing in mind that while deleting all social media accounts will\nundoubtedly make some feel infinitely better, many other people benefit from the strong\nsense of community that sharing platforms can breed.\n因此，值得记住的是：虽然删除所有社交媒体账号无疑会让一些人感觉好得多，但许多其他\n人则从共享平台所孕育的强烈社群感中获益。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nThey are conducive to promoting societal equality 借用了上一段 wider societal\nequality in the UK 的高敏感词 societal equality，但原文是说「数字排斥威胁公\n平」，并未说社交媒体平台「促进」公平。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmany other people benefit from the strong sense of community that sharing platforms can breed\n↔ They help many people feel connected with others\n预判的「连接感」和 They help many people feel connected with others 完美对\n应，且 They 指代 social media platforms / sharing platforms。\nC) 与原文相反\nThey provide a necessary device for a digital detox 颠倒了原文逻辑：even the\nconcept of a digital detox(戒瘾) requires having a device and connectivity to\nchoose to disconnect from 说的是「做数字戒瘾本身就要先有设备和网络」，设备/网\n络是 digital detox 的前提，而非由社交媒体平台提供。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey create a virtual community on the internet 用 virtual community 偷换原文的\nstrong sense of community。原文强调的是「社群归属感」（用户主观感受），而\nvirtual community 指「线上社群」这一实体，重点偏离；并且原文用 breed（孕育）\n描述的是「感受」被催生，而非「社群」被创造。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author think is really important for those living in digital exclusion?", "options": ["A) Having access to the internet.", "B) Edging further towards web 3.0.", "C) Getting more educational resources.", "D) Opening more social media accounts."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think is really important for those living in digital exclusion?\n问作者明示的观点，且原文有 crucial 一句直接对应，归为细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author think is really important for those living in digital exclusion?\nWhat 作 think 后宾语从句的主语；is really important 是表语；介词短语 for those living\nin digital exclusion 限定对象，其中 living in digital exclusion 是现在分词短语作后置定\n语修饰 those。\n【定位】It's crucial that people who feel that social media is having a detrimental effect on their\nmental health are allowed to switch off and for those living in digital exclusion to be able to\nswitch on in the first place.\n至关重要的是：让那些觉得社交媒体正在损害自己心理健康的人能够「关掉」，也要让那些\n深陷数字排斥的人首先能够「打开」。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nfor those living in digital exclusion to be able to switch on in the first place ↔\nHaving access to the internet\ngreater resources and initiatives are needed to provide the underprivileged with the connectivity\nthey desperately need\n↔ Having access to the internet\n预判「先能联网」与 Having access to the internet 完全对应，且与同段两处描述都能\n挂上。\nB) 偷换概念\nEdging further towards web 3.0 改自 Internet access will continue to grow in\nimportance as we edge further towards web 3.0，但 edge further towards web", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author think is easy to see in many areas of contemporary life?", "options": ["A) The disagreement on the inevitability of competition.", "B) The consequence of psychological investigation.", "C) The effect of human drives.", "D) The impact of competition."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干What does the author think is easy to see in many areas of contemporary life?问\n的是作者在\"当代生活的许多领域\"中明确认为容易见到的东西，属于对原文某一具体表述的\n复述题，没有 infer/imply/mainly about 等推断或主旨信号。\n【拆句】Q51主干是 What ... is easy to see；the author think 是插入的宾语从句性插入语，问的\n实际是 What is easy to see；in many areas of contemporary life 是地点状语，限定范\n围。句式平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】Whatever it is, you cannot but recognize the effect competition is exerting in academics and\nmany other areas of contemporary life.\n不管竞争是什么（天生还是习得），你都不得不承认竞争正在学术界以及当代生活的许多其\n他领域中所产生的影响。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThe disagreement on the inevitability of competition.借的是Psychologists have\nlong been in disagreement as to whether competition is a learned or a genetic\ncomponent of human behavior这一句，但原文说\"心理学家一直有分歧\"，而题干问的\n是\"你不得不看到\"的东西，把分歧与显而易见混为一谈；而且原文的分歧点是 learned\nor genetic（天生还是习得），不是 inevitability（必然性），偷换了概念。\nB) 无中生有\nThe consequence of psychological investigation.中 psychological investigation 在\nP1里找不到对应——第 1 段只提到 psychologists in disagreement，没有提任何\ninvestigation 的 consequence；且 consequence（后果）也不是原文 effect 的对应\n词。属凭空捏造。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThe effect of human drives.借了human drives这个词，但 human drives 出现在第 2\n段心理学内部解释里，是\"竞争\"的原因之一；题干问的是\"在当代生活各领域中容易见到\n的东西\"，对应第 1 段尾句，而非第 2 段。属张冠李戴。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe effect competition is exerting in academics and many other areas of contemporary life ↔\nThe impact of competition.\n预判落在\"竞争在当代生活中产生的影响\"上，The impact of competition.就是它的最简\n同义改写，词义、对象、范围都对得上。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "According to psychology, what do people strive to do following the initial stage of their life?", "options": ["A) Fulfill individual needs without incurring adverse effects of human drives.", "B) Indulge in cultural pursuits while keeping their base impulses at bay.", "C) Gain extensive recognition without exposing pure indulgence.", "D) Satisfy their own desires while observing social conventions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干According to psychology, what do people strive to do following the initial stage\nof their life?问\"心理学认为人在初始阶段之后努力做什么\"，要的是具体行为描述，属于细\n节题。\n【拆句】Q52主干是 what do people strive to do；According to psychology 是来源状语；\nfollowing the initial stage of their life 是介词短语作时间状语。strive to do = 努力去\n做，注意题干问的是\"努力的内容\"而不是\"努力的结果\"。\n【定位】Thereafter, we are at the mercy of a battle between our base impulses for self-fulfillment and\nsocial and cultural norms which prohibit pure indulgence.\n在那之后，我们便受制于一场拉锯战——一边是我们自我满足的本能冲动，另一边是禁止纯\n粹放纵的社会与文化规范。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nFulfill individual needs without incurring adverse effects of human drives.把原文\n中的两个对立面（本能冲动 vs 社会规范）改写成\"满足需求且不引发本能冲动的负面效\n应\"——但原文里 human drives 本身是积极的追求自我满足的力量，不是\"会带来负面\n后果的东西\"；且原文中真正的对立面是 social/cultural norms，与 human drives 无\n关。属与原文相反兼偷换概念。\nB) 与原文相反\nIndulge in cultural pursuits while keeping their base impulses at bay.中\"沉湎于文\n化追求、压制本能冲动\"完全把原文反过来——原文说的是文化规范禁止放纵、人的本能\n冲动想要放纵；选项却说人沉湎在文化里、压制本能。属与原文相反。\nC) 张冠李戴\nGain extensive recognition without exposing pure indulgence.把\"获得广泛认可\"放\n在答案核心——但 recognition 对应的是we compete for the attention of our\nparents的初始阶段，是题干所问\"之后\"的前一阶段，时段错位；且 extensive（广泛）\n也是对 attention of our parents 的过度扩大。属张冠李戴。\nD) ✓ 正确\nbase impulses for self-fulfillment ↔ Satisfy their own desires\nsocial and cultural norms which prohibit pure indulgence ↔\nwhile observing social conventions\n预判要求同时包含\"追求自我欲望\"和\"受社会规范约束\"两端，且两端关系是同时进行\n的\"边追求边受约束\"；Satisfy their own desires while observing social conventions.\n的 while 把两端缝在一起，与原文 a battle between ... and ... 等值。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn about the \"natural world\" characterized by Thomas Hobbes?", "options": ["A) It gets misrepresented by philosophers and anthropologists.", "B) It gets distorted in Darwin's The Origin of Species.", "C) It is free from the rational intervention of humans.", "D) It is the pre-rational world rarely appreciated nowadays."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干What do we learn about the \"natural world\" characterized by Thomas Hobbes?\n问\"我们从原文中了解到 Hobbes 所刻画的自然世界是什么\"，要的是对术语含义的复述，属\n于细节题。\n【拆句】Q53主干是 What do we learn about the \"natural world\"；characterized by Thomas\nHobbes 是过去分词短语作后置定语，修饰 natural world，含义=\"由 Hobbes 所刻画\n的\"。这里要扣的是 Hobbes 给 natural world 下的定义，不是后人的评价。\n【定位】Thomas Hobbes, one of the great philosophers of the seventeenth century, is perhaps best\nremembered for his characterization of the \"natural world,\" that is, the world before the\nimposition of the will of humanity, as being \"nasty, brutish, and short.\"\nThomas Hobbes 这位 17 世纪的大哲学家，最为人记得的或许是他对\"自然世界\"——也就\n是人类意志介入之前的世界——的刻画：\"令人厌恶、野蛮且短暂\"。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt gets misrepresented by philosophers and anthropologists.说自然世界被哲学家\n和人类学家\"歪曲表述\"——但原文只说 Hobbes 这一形象\"被达尔文的工作强\n化\"（reinforced by Charles Darwin's highly influential work），后来又说当代人类\n学研究\"质疑了这一观点\"（questioned），并没有说 Hobbes 关于 natural world 的描\n述被哲学家和人类学家共同歪曲。属无中生有。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt gets distorted in Darwin's The Origin of Species.说自然世界在达尔文的书里被歪\n曲——但原文是This image of the pre-rational world is still widely held, reinforced\nby Charles Darwin's highly influential work，达尔文是\"加强\"而非\"歪曲\"，方向相\n反。属与原文相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe world before the imposition of the will of humanity ↔\nIt is free from the rational intervention of humans.\n预判要求答案体现\"无人类理性介入\"这一特征；It is free from the rational\nintervention of humans.正是同位语的直接同义改写——free from 完美匹配 before\n（按时间顺序的不及）。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt is the pre-rational world rarely appreciated nowadays.说 pre-rational world\"当\n今很少受到欣赏\"——但原文紧接着说This image of the pre-rational world is still\nwidely held，明确说这一形象\"仍被广泛持有\"，与选项的 rarely 完全对立。属与原文\n相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What can we conclude from Darwin's assertion in The Origin of Species?", "options": ["A) All species inherently depend on others for survival.", "B) Struggles for survival do not exclude mutual support.", "C) Competition weighs as much as cooperation as a survival strategy.", "D) The strongest species proves to be the fittest in natural selection."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题）\n【判型】题干What can we conclude from Darwin's assertion in The Origin of Species?用\nconclude from 引导，要求从达尔文的断言中得出结论，属于\"一步推断题\"。\n【拆句】Q54主干是 What can we conclude from Darwin's assertion；in The Origin of Species\n是地点状语限定该断言出自哪本书。conclude from 重在\"从断言本身推出\"，所以答案必须\n紧贴 Darwin 那句原话，不能拿别的句子做依据。\n【定位】Darwin asserted in The Origin of Species that the struggles he was describing should be\nviewed as metaphors and could easily include dependence and cooperation.\n达尔文在《物种起源》中断言：他所描述的那些斗争应当被视为隐喻，完全可以涵盖依赖与\n合作。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nAll species inherently depend on others for survival.中 All species（所有物种）+\ninherently（天生地）是绝对化用语；原文只是说\"斗争可以包含依赖与合作\"，并未推\n出\"所有物种都天生依赖他者才能生存\"。属过度推断。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe struggles he was describing should be viewed as metaphors and could easily include\ndependence and cooperation\n↔ Struggles for survival do not exclude mutual support.\n预判说斗争不排斥互助；Struggles for survival do not exclude mutual support.中\ndo not exclude 与原文 could include 等值，mutual support 概括 dependence and\ncooperation，逻辑等值、范围一致。\nC) 过度推断\nCompetition weighs as much as cooperation as a survival strategy.中\"竞争与合作\n作为生存策略权重相当\"，把两者并列等值——但原文只说斗争\"可以包含\"合作，并未\n在\"权重相当\"的层面比较两者；权重比较是凭空引入。属过度推断。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe strongest species proves to be the fittest in natural selection.说\"最强的物种被\n证明是最适合的\"——但原文的关键点是\"survival of the fittest 这个短语达尔文自己从\n未使用\"，且本句要的是\"Darwin's assertion\"（达尔文的断言），不是社会上对\"适者生\n存\"的概括；况且 fittest 也不等于 strongest，原文专门指出自然选择并不暗示竞争才是\n最优策略。属偷换概念。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What conclusion did Margaret Mead reach after studying dozens of different cultures?", "options": ["A) It is characteristic of humans to be competitive.", "B) Americans are uniquely opposed to cooperation.", "C) Competition is relatively more prevalent in Western societies.", "D) People's attitude towards competition is actually culture-bound."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干What conclusion did Margaret Mead reach after studying dozens of different\ncultures?问\"Mead 得出了什么结论\"，是对原文最后一句明文表达的结论的复述，属于细\n节题。\n【拆句】Q55主干是 What conclusion did Margaret Mead reach；after studying dozens of\ndifferent cultures 是时间状语。注意问的是\"Mead 的结论\"，所以答案必须是 Mead's\nfinal conclusion 那一句的同义改写，而不是 Mead 研究过程的具体发现（那是中间结论，\n不是 final conclusion）。\n【定位】After studying dozens of such cultures, Mead's final conclusion was that competitiveness is a\nculturally created aspect of human behavior, and that its prevalence in a particular society is\nrelative to how that society values it.\n在研究了数十个这样的文化之后，Mead 的最终结论是：竞争性是人类行为中由文化塑造出\n的一面；它在某个具体社会中的普遍程度，取决于该社会对它的评价。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt is characteristic of humans to be competitive.说\"竞争是人类的天性\"——但这正是\nMead 想要驳斥的观点，Mead 的结论恰恰相反：竞争性不是天生的，而是文化塑造\n的。属与原文相反。\nB) 偷换概念\nAmericans are uniquely opposed to cooperation.说美国人\"独一无二地反对合\n作\"——但原文是Americans uniquely praise competition as natural, inevitable, and\ndesirable，说美国人独一无二地\"赞美竞争\"，并未说他们反对合作；\"赞美 X\"与\"反对\nY\"不可直接对调。属偷换概念。\nC) 过度推断\nCompetition is relatively more prevalent in Western societies.说\"竞争在西方社会中\n相对更普遍\"——但 Mead 的最终结论是\"竞争普遍程度取决于社会评价\"，并未把社会\n分成\"西方/非西方\"两类、也未声称\"竞争在西方更普遍\"是普遍规律。属过度推断兼以偏\n概全。\nD) ✓ 正确\ncompetitiveness is a culturally created aspect of human behavior ↔\nPeople's attitude towards competition is actually culture-bound.\nits prevalence in a particular society is relative to how that society values it ↔\nactually culture-bound\n预判要求答案体现\"竞争性受文化决定\"；People's attitude towards competition is\nactually culture-bound.中 culture-bound 直接等值于 culturally created，是 Mead\n结论的最简同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
